window.FLASHCARDS_EN_KAPITEL_5 = {
  "k5_def_5_1": {
    title: "Definition 5.1: Norms",
    category: "5.1 Normed vector spaces",
    front: "What is the precise definition of a <b>norm</b> $\\|\\cdot\\|$ on a vector space $V$ over $\\mathbb{K} = \\mathbb{R}$ (or $\\mathbb{K} = \\mathbb{C}$)?",
    back: "A <b>norm</b> on $V$ is a map $\\|\\cdot\\|: v \\in V \\mapsto \\|v\\| \\in \\mathbb{R}_{\\ge 0}$ with the following three properties:<br><br>1. <b>Definiteness:</b> For all $v \\in V$<br>$$\\|v\\| = 0 \\iff v = 0$$<br>2. <b>Homogeneity:</b> For all $v \\in V$ and $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{K}$<br>$$\\|\\alpha v\\| = |\\alpha| \\cdot \\|v\\|$$<br>3. <b>Triangle inequality:</b> For all $v_1, v_2 \\in V$<br>$$\\|v_1 + v_2\\| \\le \\|v_1\\| + \\|v_2\\|$$<br><br>$V$ together with $\\|\\cdot\\|$ is called a <b>normed vector space</b>.",
    meta: "Script: Definition 5.1, p. 225",
    explanation: "<b>Intuition of the axioms:</b><br>&bull; <i>Definiteness:</i> only the zero vector has length $0$; every other vector has strictly positive length.<br>&bull; <i>Homogeneity:</i> scaling a vector by $\\alpha$ scales its length by $|\\alpha|$.<br>&bull; <i>Triangle inequality:</i> the direct route ($v_1 + v_2$) is never longer than the detour through the individual steps.<br><br><svg width='100%' height='130' viewBox='0 0 320 130' style='max-width:100%; height:auto; background:#f8fafc; border-radius:8px; margin-top:8px;'><line x1='30' y1='100' x2='150' y2='30' stroke='#1f407a' stroke-width='2.5'/><line x1='150' y1='30' x2='270' y2='100' stroke='#10b981' stroke-width='2.5'/><line x1='30' y1='100' x2='270' y2='100' stroke='#ef4444' stroke-dasharray='4 4' stroke-width='2.5'/><text x='80' y='55' fill='#1f407a' font-weight='bold' font-size='12'>||v&#8321;||</text><text x='220' y='55' fill='#10b981' font-weight='bold' font-size='12'>||v&#8322;||</text><text x='130' y='120' fill='#ef4444' font-weight='bold' font-size='12'>||v&#8321;+v&#8322;|| &#8804; ||v&#8321;||+||v&#8322;||</text></svg>",
    example: "The simplest example: $\\mathbb{R}$ (as a 1-dimensional vector space over $\\mathbb{R}$) with the absolute value $|\\cdot|$ is a normed vector space. Likewise $\\mathbb{C}$ with the absolute value (as a vector space over $\\mathbb{R}$ or $\\mathbb{C}$).",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> $f(x) = x^2$ on $\\mathbb{R}$ is <b>not</b> a norm: homogeneity fails, since $f(2x) = 4x^2 \\neq 2f(x) = |2| \\cdot f(x)$.<br><b>2.</b> $f(v) = |v_1|$ on $\\mathbb{R}^2$ is not a norm: definiteness fails, since $f((0,1)^t) = 0$ although $(0,1)^t \\neq 0$ (such a function is called only a <i>seminorm</i>)."
  },
  "k5_bsp_5_2": {
    title: "Example 5.2: Maximum norm and 1-norm",
    category: "5.1 Normed vector spaces",
    front: "How are the <b>maximum norm $\\|v\\|_\\infty$</b> and the <b>1-norm $\\|v\\|_1$</b> defined on $\\mathbb{C}^d$ and $\\mathbb{R}^d$?",
    back: "For $j \\in \\{1,\\dots,d\\}$ let $\\pi_j: v = (v_1,\\dots,v_d)^t \\in \\mathbb{C}^d \\mapsto v_j \\in \\mathbb{C}$ be the <b>projection</b> onto the $j$-th component. Then:<br><br>&bull; <b>Maximum norm (infinity norm):</b><br>$$\\|v\\|_\\infty = \\max_{j=1,\\dots,d} |\\pi_j(v)|$$<br>&bull; <b>1-norm:</b><br>$$\\|v\\|_1 = \\sum_{j=1}^d |\\pi_j(v)|$$<br><br>On $\\mathbb{R}^d$ both are defined by the same formulas.",
    meta: "Script: Example 5.2, p. 226",
    explanation: "<b>Geometric comparison of the unit balls in $\\mathbb{R}^2$:</b><br>&bull; <i>Maximum norm:</i> only measures the largest component in absolute value &rarr; the unit ball is an axis-parallel square.<br>&bull; <i>1-norm:</i> adds up all absolute values of the components &rarr; the unit ball is a diamond standing on its tip.<br><br><svg width='100%' height='150' viewBox='0 0 320 150' style='max-width:100%; height:auto; background:#f8fafc; border-radius:8px; margin-top:8px;'><rect x='30' y='25' width='90' height='90' fill='none' stroke='#ef4444' stroke-width='2'/><polygon points='230,25 275,70 230,115 185,70' fill='none' stroke='#10b981' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='75' cy='70' r='45' fill='none' stroke='#1f407a' stroke-dasharray='3 3' stroke-width='1.5'/><circle cx='230' cy='70' r='45' fill='none' stroke='#1f407a' stroke-dasharray='3 3' stroke-width='1.5'/><text x='75' y='135' text-anchor='middle' font-size='11' fill='#444'>||v||&#8734; (square)</text><text x='230' y='135' text-anchor='middle' font-size='11' fill='#444'>||v||&#8321; (diamond)</text></svg>",
    example: "For $v = (3, -4)^t \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$:<br>$$\\|v\\|_\\infty = \\max(|3|, |-4|) = 4, \\qquad \\|v\\|_1 = |3| + |-4| = 7, \\qquad \\|v\\|_2 = \\sqrt{9+16} = 5.$$",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> In general $\\|v\\|_1 \\neq \\|v\\|_\\infty$ &ndash; only for $d = 1$ do all three norms agree (then they are the absolute value).<br><b>2.</b> $v \\mapsto \\min_j |\\pi_j(v)|$ is <i>not</i> a norm on $\\mathbb{R}^d$ ($d \\ge 2$): $(1, 0)^t$ would have &ldquo;length&rdquo; $0$ &ndash; definiteness fails."
  },
  "k5_euklid_skalarprodukt": {
    title: "5.1.1 The Euclidean inner product",
    category: "5.1 Normed vector spaces",
    front: "How is the <b>Euclidean inner product (scalar product)</b> on $\\mathbb{C}^d$ defined, and which three properties does it satisfy?",
    back: "For $v = (v_1,\\dots,v_d)^t,\\ w = (w_1,\\dots,w_d)^t \\in \\mathbb{C}^d$:<br>$$\\langle v, w \\rangle = \\sum_{k=1}^d v_k \\overline{w_k}$$<br><b>Properties:</b><br>1. <b>Sesquilinearity:</b> linear in the 1st argument, &ldquo;semi-linear&rdquo; (conjugate-linear) in the 2nd argument: $\\langle v, \\alpha_1 w_1 + \\alpha_2 w_2 \\rangle = \\overline{\\alpha_1}\\langle v, w_1\\rangle + \\overline{\\alpha_2}\\langle v, w_2\\rangle$.<br>2. <b>Symmetry:</b> $\\langle v, w \\rangle = \\overline{\\langle w, v \\rangle}$<br>3. <b>Definiteness:</b> $\\langle v, v \\rangle \\ge 0$, and $\\langle v, v \\rangle = 0$ exactly when $v = 0$.<br><br>The <b>real inner product</b> on $\\mathbb{R}^d$ is defined by the same formula and is <b>bilinear</b> (linear in both arguments).",
    meta: "Script: Section 5.1.1, pp. 226-227",
    explanation: "<b>Why the complex conjugation $\\overline{w_k}$ is needed:</b> without it, $\\langle (i,0)^t, (i,0)^t \\rangle = i \\cdot i = -1 < 0$ &ndash; definiteness would break down. With conjugation, $\\langle v, v \\rangle = \\sum_k |v_k|^2 \\ge 0$, and $\\sum_k |v_k|^2 = 0$ forces $v_k = 0$ for all $k$.<br><br>&ldquo;Sesqui&rdquo; means <i>one and a half</i>: once linear plus &ldquo;half&rdquo; linear.",
    example: "In $\\mathbb{C}^2$: for $v = (1, i)^t$ and $w = (2, 1-i)^t$:<br>$$\\langle v, w \\rangle = 1 \\cdot \\overline{2} + i \\cdot \\overline{(1-i)} = 2 + i(1+i) = 2 + i - 1 = 1 + i.$$",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> The formula $\\sum_k v_k w_k$ (without conjugation) is <i>not</i> an inner product on $\\mathbb{C}^d$: $\\langle (i,0)^t, (i,0)^t \\rangle = -1$ violates definiteness.<br><b>2.</b> The inner product is <i>not</i> linear in the 2nd argument: $\\langle v, i w \\rangle = \\overline{i}\\langle v, w\\rangle = -i\\langle v, w\\rangle \\neq i \\langle v, w \\rangle$ (unless $\\langle v,w\\rangle = 0$)."
  },
  "k5_prop_5_3": {
    title: "Proposition 5.3: Cauchy-Schwarz inequality",
    category: "5.1 Normed vector spaces",
    front: "What is the <b>Cauchy-Schwarz inequality</b> on $\\mathbb{C}^d$ (resp. $\\mathbb{R}^d$), and when does equality hold?",
    back: "For all $v, w \\in \\mathbb{C}^d$:<br>$$|\\langle v, w \\rangle| \\le \\|v\\| \\, \\|w\\|$$<br>Equality holds <b>if and only if</b> $v$ and $w$ are <b>linearly dependent</b> (i.e. there is $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C}$ with $\\alpha v = w$ or $v = \\alpha w$).",
    meta: "Script: Proposition 5.3, pp. 227-228",
    explanation: "<b>Meaning:</b> the inner product of two vectors can be controlled by their lengths. Over the reals this allows us to define the angle $\\theta$ between vectors via $\\cos(\\theta) = \\frac{\\langle v, w \\rangle}{\\|v\\|_2 \\|w\\|_2} \\in [-1,1]$.<br><br><b>Remark from the script:</b> the proof uses <i>only</i> the three axioms of the inner product (sesquilinearity, symmetry, definiteness), not the concrete formula &ndash; so the inequality holds in every vector space with an inner product.",
    example: "For $v = (1, 2)^t$, $w = (2, 4)^t = 2v$ (linearly dependent): $|\\langle v, w \\rangle| = |2 + 8| = 10 = \\sqrt{5} \\cdot \\sqrt{20} = \\|v\\|\\|w\\|$ &ndash; equality.",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> For linearly independent vectors the inequality is <i>strict</i>: $v = (1,0)^t$, $w = (1,1)^t$ gives $|\\langle v, w\\rangle| = 1 < \\sqrt{2} = \\|v\\|\\|w\\|$.<br><b>2.</b> Without the absolute value on the left the statement would be weaker or useless in the wrong direction: over the reals $\\langle v, w \\rangle$ can be negative (e.g. $v = (1,0)^t$, $w = (-1,0)^t$: $\\langle v,w\\rangle = -1$); the bound holds for the <i>absolute value</i>."
  },
  "k5_beweis_5_3": {
    title: "Proof: Cauchy-Schwarz inequality",
    category: "5.1 Normed vector spaces",
    front: "Sketch the proof of the <b>Cauchy-Schwarz inequality</b> (Proposition 5.3).",
    back: "<b>Approach (orthogonal projection):</b> If $v = 0$ or $w = 0$, both sides are $0$. So let $w \\neq 0$ and $\\alpha = \\frac{\\langle v, w \\rangle}{\\|w\\|^2}$.<br><br>1. Expanding with sesquilinearity:<br>$$0 \\le \\|v - \\alpha w\\|^2 = \\|v\\|^2 - \\overline{\\alpha}\\langle v, w \\rangle - \\alpha\\overline{\\langle v, w \\rangle} + |\\alpha|^2 \\|w\\|^2$$<br>2. Substituting $\\alpha$:<br>$$= \\|v\\|^2 - 2\\frac{|\\langle v,w \\rangle|^2}{\\|w\\|^2} + \\frac{|\\langle v,w \\rangle|^2}{\\|w\\|^2} = \\|v\\|^2 - \\frac{|\\langle v,w \\rangle|^2}{\\|w\\|^2}$$<br>3. Hence $|\\langle v, w \\rangle|^2 \\le \\|v\\|^2 \\|w\\|^2$; taking square roots gives the claim.<br><br><b>Equality case:</b> equality holds exactly when $\\|v - \\alpha w\\| = 0$, i.e. $v = \\alpha w$. $\\square$",
    meta: "Script: proof of Proposition 5.3, p. 228",
    explanation: "<b>Geometric intuition:</b> $\\alpha w$ is the orthogonal projection of $v$ onto the line spanned by $w$; $v - \\alpha w$ is the &ldquo;perpendicular&rdquo;. That its squared length is $\\ge 0$ <i>is</i> already the inequality.<br><br><svg width='100%' height='130' viewBox='0 0 320 130' style='max-width:100%; height:auto; background:#f8fafc; border-radius:8px; margin-top:8px;'><line x1='30' y1='105' x2='280' y2='105' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='2'/><line x1='30' y1='105' x2='230' y2='30' stroke='#1f407a' stroke-width='2.5'/><line x1='30' y1='105' x2='180' y2='105' stroke='#10b981' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='230' y1='30' x2='180' y2='105' stroke='#ef4444' stroke-dasharray='4 4' stroke-width='2'/><text x='115' y='60' fill='#1f407a' font-weight='bold' font-size='12'>v</text><text x='105' y='122' fill='#10b981' font-weight='bold' font-size='12'>&#945;w</text><text x='215' y='75' fill='#ef4444' font-weight='bold' font-size='12'>v &#8722; &#945;w</text></svg>",
    example: "Alternative (Exercise 5.4): the Cauchy inequality with $\\varepsilon$ gives a second proof by choosing $\\varepsilon$ optimally.",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> The trick $\\|v - \\alpha w\\|^2 \\ge 0$ only works with the <i>optimal</i> projection $\\alpha = \\frac{\\langle v,w\\rangle}{\\|w\\|^2}$ &ndash; with e.g. $\\alpha = 1$ you only get the (weaker) statement $2\\operatorname{Re}\\langle v,w\\rangle \\le \\|v\\|^2 + \\|w\\|^2$.<br><b>2.</b> For $w = 0$ one must not divide by $\\|w\\|^2$ &ndash; this case has to be treated separately (trivially); a common proof error."
  },
  "k5_uebung_5_4": {
    title: "Exercise 5.4: Cauchy inequality with an epsilon",
    category: "5.1 Normed vector spaces",
    front: "What is the <b>Cauchy inequality with $\\varepsilon$</b> for $v, w \\in \\mathbb{R}^d$ and $\\varepsilon > 0$?",
    back: "For every $\\varepsilon > 0$ and all $v, w \\in \\mathbb{R}^d$:<br>$$|\\langle v, w \\rangle| \\le \\frac{\\varepsilon^2}{2} \\|v\\|^2 + \\frac{1}{2\\varepsilon^2} \\|w\\|^2$$<br>From this the Cauchy-Schwarz inequality (5.1) can be deduced.",
    meta: "Script: Exercise 5.4, p. 228",
    explanation: "<b>Proof trick:</b> positivity of the norm applied to $\\left\\|\\varepsilon v \\mp \\tfrac{1}{\\varepsilon} w\\right\\|^2 \\ge 0$; expanding gives<br>$$\\varepsilon^2 \\|v\\|^2 \\mp 2\\langle v, w \\rangle + \\tfrac{1}{\\varepsilon^2}\\|w\\|^2 \\ge 0.$$<br><b>Way back to Cauchy-Schwarz:</b> for $v, w \\neq 0$ minimise the right-hand side over $\\varepsilon$: with $\\varepsilon^2 = \\frac{\\|w\\|}{\\|v\\|}$ it becomes exactly $\\|v\\|\\|w\\|$. This &ldquo;$\\varepsilon$-form&rdquo; of an inequality is a pattern found everywhere in analysis (Young's inequality).",
    example: "With $\\varepsilon = 1$: $|\\langle v, w \\rangle| \\le \\tfrac12\\|v\\|^2 + \\tfrac12\\|w\\|^2$ &ndash; the arithmetic mean of the squared norms; special case $ab \\le \\tfrac{a^2+b^2}{2}$ for real numbers.",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> For fixed $\\varepsilon$ the bound is in general <i>not</i> sharp: for $v = w = (1,0)^t$ and $\\varepsilon^2 = 2$ it gives $1 \\le 1 + \\tfrac14 = 1.25$.<br><b>2.</b> Without the second term $\\frac{1}{2\\varepsilon^2}\\|w\\|^2$ the statement would be false: $|\\langle v,w\\rangle| \\le \\frac{\\varepsilon^2}{2}\\|v\\|^2$ fails for small $\\varepsilon$ (take $v = w \\neq 0$). The two terms balance each other."
  },
  "k5_korollar_5_5": {
    title: "Corollary 5.5: The Euclidean norm is a norm",
    category: "5.1 Normed vector spaces",
    front: "How is the <b>Euclidean norm (2-norm)</b> on $\\mathbb{C}^d$ defined, and how does one prove its triangle inequality?",
    back: "$$\\|v\\| = \\|v\\|_2 = \\sqrt{\\langle v, v \\rangle} = \\sqrt{\\sum_{k=1}^d |v_k|^2}$$<br><b>Corollary 5.5:</b> It defines a norm on $\\mathbb{C}^d$.<br><br><b>Proof of the triangle inequality:</b><br>$$\\|v+w\\|^2 = \\langle v+w, v+w \\rangle = \\|v\\|^2 + 2\\operatorname{Re}(\\langle v,w \\rangle) + \\|w\\|^2$$<br>With $\\operatorname{Re}(\\langle v,w\\rangle) \\le |\\langle v,w \\rangle| \\le \\|v\\|\\|w\\|$ (Cauchy-Schwarz):<br>$$\\|v+w\\|^2 \\le \\|v\\|^2 + 2\\|v\\|\\|w\\| + \\|w\\|^2 = (\\|v\\| + \\|w\\|)^2,$$<br>taking square roots gives the claim. $\\square$",
    meta: "Script: Corollary 5.5, p. 228",
    explanation: "Definiteness and homogeneity follow directly from the properties of the inner product ($\\langle \\alpha v, \\alpha v \\rangle = |\\alpha|^2 \\langle v,v\\rangle$). The triangle inequality is the non-trivial part &ndash; it stands and falls with Cauchy-Schwarz. Restricting to $\\mathbb{R}^d \\subseteq \\mathbb{C}^d$ yields the Euclidean inner product and the Euclidean norm on $\\mathbb{R}^d$; all statements hold there analogously.",
    example: "$v = (3,4)^t \\in \\mathbb{R}^2$: $\\|v\\|_2 = \\sqrt{9 + 16} = 5$ &ndash; the classical Pythagorean theorem as a special case.",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> Without the detour via $\\|v+w\\|^2$ things go wrong: taking square roots of $\\sum|v_k + w_k|^2$ term by term cannot be estimated directly &ndash; the quadratic trick with $\\operatorname{Re}$ is essential.<br><b>2.</b> $\\sqrt{\\sum_k v_k^2}$ (without absolute values) is not a norm on $\\mathbb{C}^d$: for $v = (i, 0)^t$ we would have $\\sum v_k^2 = -1$, and the square root is not even real."
  },
  "k5_raum_stetiger_fkt": {
    title: "5.1.2 Norms on the function space C([a,b])",
    category: "5.1 Normed vector spaces",
    front: "How are the <b>supremum norm $\\|f\\|_\\infty$</b> and the <b>1-norm $\\|f\\|_1$</b> defined on the vector space $C([a,b])$ of continuous functions?",
    back: "Let $K = [a,b]$ with $a < b$ be compact and $V = C([a,b])$ the vector space of continuous real-valued functions on $[a,b]$.<br><br>&bull; <b>Supremum norm (Example 5.7):</b><br>$$\\|f\\|_\\infty = \\sup_{x \\in [a,b]} |f(x)| = \\max_{x \\in [a,b]} |f(x)|$$<br>(the maximum is attained by Theorem 3.67)<br><br>&bull; <b>1-norm (Example 5.8):</b><br>$$\\|f\\|_1 = \\int_a^b |f(x)|\\,\\mathrm{d}x$$<br>(well-defined, since $|f|$ is continuous (Prop. 3.52) and continuous functions are Riemann integrable (Theorem 4.42))",
    meta: "Script: Exercise 5.6, Examples 5.7 & 5.8, Exercise 5.9, p. 229",
    explanation: "<b>Why function spaces?</b> $C([a,b])$ is <i>infinite-dimensional</i> (Exercise 5.6) &ndash; analysis needs norms beyond $\\mathbb{R}^d$, for example for differential equations.<br><b>Comparison:</b> $\\|f\\|_\\infty$ controls the maximal deviation (&rarr; uniform convergence), $\\|f\\|_1$ the area under $|f|$ (&rarr; convergence in the mean).<br><br><svg width='100%' height='130' viewBox='0 0 320 130' style='max-width:100%; height:auto; background:#f8fafc; border-radius:8px; margin-top:8px;'><path d='M30,100 Q100,10 160,80 T290,100' fill='none' stroke='#1f407a' stroke-width='2.5'/><line x1='30' y1='100' x2='290' y2='100' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='1.5'/><line x1='105' y1='32' x2='105' y2='100' stroke='#ef4444' stroke-dasharray='3 3' stroke-width='2'/><text x='112' y='45' fill='#ef4444' font-size='11' font-weight='bold'>||f||&#8734; (peak)</text><path d='M30,100 Q100,10 160,80 T290,100 Z' fill='#10b981' fill-opacity='0.2'/><text x='200' y='85' fill='#10b981' font-size='11' font-weight='bold'>||f||&#8321; (area)</text></svg>",
    example: "For $f(x) = x$ on $[0,1]$: $\\|f\\|_\\infty = 1$ and $\\|f\\|_1 = \\int_0^1 x\\,\\mathrm{d}x = \\tfrac12$. Triangle inequality for $\\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty$: $\\max|f_1 + f_2| \\le \\max(|f_1| + |f_2|) \\le \\max|f_1| + \\max|f_2|$.",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> On the larger space of all <i>Riemann integrable</i> functions $\\|f\\|_1 = \\int_a^b |f|\\,\\mathrm{d}x$ is no longer a norm: the function $f = \\mathbb{1}_{\\{a\\}}$ (value 1 only at the point $a$) satisfies $\\|f\\|_1 = 0$, but is not the zero function &ndash; definiteness needs continuity!<br><b>2.</b> The two norms are genuinely different: there are sequences $(f_n)_n$ with $\\|f_n\\|_1 \\to 0$ but $\\|f_n\\|_\\infty = 1$ for all $n$ (narrow, tall triangular spikes; Exercise 5.47)."
  },

  "k5_def_5_10": {
    title: "Definition 5.10: Metric and metric space",
    category: "5.2 Metric spaces",
    front: "How are a <b>metric space $(X, \\mathrm{d})$</b> and a <b>metric</b> formally defined?",
    back: "A <b>metric space</b> $(X, \\mathrm{d})$ is a set $X$ together with a map $\\mathrm{d}: X \\times X \\to \\mathbb{R}_{\\ge 0}$ (the <b>metric</b>) which satisfies, for all $x_1, x_2, x_3 \\in X$:<br><br>1. <b>Definiteness:</b> $\\mathrm{d}(x_1, x_2) = 0 \\iff x_1 = x_2$<br>2. <b>Symmetry:</b> $\\mathrm{d}(x_1, x_2) = \\mathrm{d}(x_2, x_1)$<br>3. <b>Triangle inequality:</b> $\\mathrm{d}(x_1, x_3) \\le \\mathrm{d}(x_1, x_2) + \\mathrm{d}(x_2, x_3)$",
    meta: "Script: Definition 5.10, p. 230",
    explanation: "A metric assigns to any two points their <b>distance</b> &ndash; with no vector space structure at all. That is why one can measure distances on arbitrary sets (graphs, function spaces, words, ...). The triangle inequality says: the shortest path from $x_1$ to $x_3$ is at most as long as the detour via $x_2$.<br><br>For a subset $Y \\subseteq X$ the restriction $\\mathrm{d}|_{Y \\times Y}$ is a metric on $Y$: $Y$ is then called a <b>subspace</b> with the <b>induced metric</b>.",
    example: "$X = \\mathbb{R}$ with $\\mathrm{d}(x,y) = |x-y|$; $X = \\mathbb{C}$ with $\\mathrm{d}(z_1,z_2) = |z_1 - z_2|$; $X = \\mathbb{C}^d$ with $\\mathrm{d}_1, \\mathrm{d}_2$ or $\\mathrm{d}_\\infty$ (induced by the norms $\\|\\cdot\\|_1, \\|\\cdot\\|_2, \\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty$).",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> $\\mathrm{d}(x,y) = (x-y)^2$ on $\\mathbb{R}$ is <b>not</b> a metric: the triangle inequality fails, since $\\mathrm{d}(0,2) = 4 > \\mathrm{d}(0,1) + \\mathrm{d}(1,2) = 1 + 1 = 2$.<br><b>2.</b> $\\mathrm{d}(x,y) = |x^2 - y^2|$ on $\\mathbb{R}$ is not a metric: $\\mathrm{d}(1,-1) = 0$ although $1 \\neq -1$ &ndash; definiteness fails."
  },
  "k5_lemma_5_11": {
    title: "Lemma 5.11: A norm defines a metric",
    category: "5.2 Metric spaces",
    front: "How does a norm $\\|\\cdot\\|$ on a vector space $V$ <b>induce</b> a metric, and how does one prove the metric axioms?",
    back: "The formula $$\\mathrm{d}_{\\|\\cdot\\|}(v_1, v_2) = \\|v_1 - v_2\\|$$ defines a metric on $V$, the <b>metric induced by the norm</b>.<br><br><b>Proof:</b><br>1. <i>Definiteness:</i> $\\|v_1 - v_2\\| = 0 \\iff v_1 - v_2 = 0 \\iff v_1 = v_2$ (definiteness of the norm).<br>2. <i>Symmetry:</i> $\\|v_1 - v_2\\| = \\|(-1)(v_2 - v_1)\\| = |-1|\\,\\|v_2 - v_1\\|$ (homogeneity with $\\alpha = -1$).<br>3. <i>Triangle inequality:</i> $\\|v_1 - v_3\\| = \\|(v_1 - v_2) + (v_2 - v_3)\\| \\le \\|v_1 - v_2\\| + \\|v_2 - v_3\\|$. $\\square$",
    meta: "Script: Lemma 5.11, p. 231",
    explanation: "Every normed vector space is automatically a metric space: distance = length of the difference vector. So all later notions (balls, convergence, continuity) carry over immediately to $\\mathbb{R}^d$, $\\mathbb{C}^d$ and $C([a,b])$.<br><br><b>Hierarchy of the chapter:</b> $(\\mathbb{C}^d, \\mathbb{R}^d \\text{ with norms}) \\subsetneq$ normed vector spaces $\\subsetneq$ metric spaces.",
    example: "The Euclidean norm induces the Euclidean metric $\\mathrm{d}_2(x,y) = \\sqrt{\\sum_i (x_i - y_i)^2}$ on $\\mathbb{R}^d$.",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> The converse fails: the <i>discrete metric</i> on $\\mathbb{R}^d$ does not come from any norm, since a norm with $\\|v_2 - v_1\\| = \\mathrm{d}_{\\text{discrete}}(v_1,v_2)$ would violate homogeneity ($\\mathrm{d}(0, 2v) = 1 \\neq 2 = 2\\,\\mathrm{d}(0,v)$ for $v \\neq 0$).<br><b>2.</b> Metrics also exist where there is no vector space at all (graphs, the SNCF metric on half-lines) &ndash; there the question &ldquo;induced by a norm&rdquo; does not even make sense."
  },
  "k5_bsp_5_12_metriken": {
    title: "Example 5.12: Discrete, Manhattan and SNCF metrics",
    category: "5.2 Metric spaces",
    front: "Describe the <b>discrete metric</b>, the <b>Manhattan metric</b> and the <b>SNCF metric</b> (metric of the French railway).",
    back: "&bull; <b>Discrete metric</b> (on an arbitrary set $X$):<br>$$\\mathrm{d}_{\\text{discrete}}(x_1, x_2) = \\begin{cases} 1 & \\text{if } x_1 \\neq x_2 \\\\ 0 & \\text{if } x_1 = x_2 \\end{cases}$$<br>&bull; <b>Manhattan metric</b> on $X = [0,1]^2$:<br>$$\\mathrm{d}_{\\text{NY}}((x_1,y_1),(x_2,y_2)) = |x_1-x_2| + |y_1-y_2|$$<br>(= restriction of the 1-metric $\\mathrm{d}_1$ on $\\mathbb{R}^2$)<br>&bull; <b>SNCF metric</b> on $X = \\mathbb{C}$:<br>$$\\mathrm{d}_{\\text{SNCF}}(z_1, z_2) = \\begin{cases} |z_1 - z_2| & \\text{if } z_1, z_2 \\text{ are linearly dependent over } \\mathbb{R} \\\\ |z_1| + |z_2| & \\text{if linearly independent} \\end{cases}$$",
    meta: "Script: Example 5.12 & Exercise 5.13, pp. 231-233",
    explanation: "<b>Intuition:</b> Manhattan: on a grid of city streets you first walk along one coordinate, then the other. SNCF: every train journey from $z_1$ to $z_2$ goes via the origin (&ldquo;Paris&rdquo;), unless both points lie on the same line leaving Paris &ndash; $\\mathbb{C}$ splits into infinitely many half-lines that meet only at $0$.<br><br><svg width='100%' height='140' viewBox='0 0 320 140' style='max-width:100%; height:auto; background:#f8fafc; border-radius:8px;'><line x1='30' y1='110' x2='120' y2='110' stroke='#10b981' stroke-width='3'/><line x1='120' y1='110' x2='120' y2='30' stroke='#10b981' stroke-width='3'/><circle cx='30' cy='110' r='4' fill='#10b981'/><circle cx='120' cy='30' r='4' fill='#10b981'/><text x='75' y='130' text-anchor='middle' font-size='10' fill='#444'>Manhattan (grid)</text><line x1='180' y1='40' x2='230' y2='90' stroke='#ef4444' stroke-width='2'/><line x1='230' y1='90' x2='280' y2='30' stroke='#ef4444' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='230' cy='90' r='5' fill='#1f407a'/><text x='230' y='105' text-anchor='middle' font-size='9' font-weight='bold' fill='#1f407a'>Paris (0)</text><text x='230' y='130' text-anchor='middle' font-size='10' fill='#444'>SNCF (via Paris)</text></svg><br>Also <b>graphs</b> (Example 5.12 (iv)) carry metrics: the distance between two vertices is the minimal number of edges of a connecting path (requires the graph to be connected).",
    example: "SNCF: $z_1 = 3i$, $z_2 = 4i$ lie on the same line through the origin &rarr; $\\mathrm{d}(z_1,z_2) = |3i - 4i| = 1$. By contrast $z_1 = 3$, $z_2 = 4i$ (linearly independent) &rarr; $\\mathrm{d}(z_1,z_2) = 3 + 4 = 7$. Discrete: $\\mathrm{d}(0{,}00001, 0) = 1$, however close the points are.",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> The discrete metric on $\\mathbb{R}^d$ is <i>not</i> induced by a norm (homogeneity fails) &ndash; metric spaces are genuinely more general than normed vector spaces.<br><b>2.</b> The Manhattan and SNCF metrics on $[0,1]^2 \\subseteq \\mathbb{C}$ are really different: there are sequences that converge with respect to $\\mathrm{d}_{\\text{NY}}$ but diverge with respect to $\\mathrm{d}_{\\text{SNCF}}$ (Exercise 5.43)."
  },
  "k5_uebung_5_14": {
    title: "Important Exercise 5.14: Reverse triangle inequality",
    category: "5.2 Metric spaces",
    front: "What is the <b>reverse triangle inequality</b> in a metric space $(X, \\mathrm{d})$, and how does one prove it?",
    back: "For all $x_1, x_2, y \\in X$:<br>$$|\\mathrm{d}(x_1, y) - \\mathrm{d}(x_2, y)| \\le \\mathrm{d}(x_1, x_2)$$<br><br><b>Proof:</b><br>1. Triangle inequality: $\\mathrm{d}(x_1, y) \\le \\mathrm{d}(x_1, x_2) + \\mathrm{d}(x_2, y)$, so $\\mathrm{d}(x_1,y) - \\mathrm{d}(x_2,y) \\le \\mathrm{d}(x_1,x_2)$.<br>2. Swap the roles $x_1 \\leftrightarrow x_2$: $\\mathrm{d}(x_2,y) - \\mathrm{d}(x_1,y) \\le \\mathrm{d}(x_1,x_2)$.<br>3. $A \\le C$ and $-A \\le C$ give $|A| \\le C$. $\\square$",
    meta: "Script: Important Exercise 5.14, p. 233",
    explanation: "<b>Meaning:</b> the distance function $x \\mapsto \\mathrm{d}(x, y)$ to a fixed point $y$ is 1-Lipschitz continuous: if the measuring point moves by $\\mathrm{d}(x_1,x_2)$, the measured distance changes by at most that amount. This is the metric analogue of $||a| - |b|| \\le |a - b|$ on $\\mathbb{R}$ (Theorem 2.46).",
    example: "In $\\mathbb{R}$: $|\\,|x_1 - y| - |x_2 - y|\\,| \\le |x_1 - x_2|$. Concretely $x_1 = 5, x_2 = 3, y = 0$: $|5 - 3| = 2 \\le 2$. More generally $x \\mapsto \\mathrm{d}(x, A)$ for subsets $A$ is also 1-Lipschitz (Important Exercise 5.54).",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> The bound cannot be improved: for $y$ on the line &ldquo;beyond&rdquo; $x_2$ (in $\\mathbb{R}$: $x_1 = 0, x_2 = 1, y = 2$) equality holds: $|2 - 1| = 1 = \\mathrm{d}(x_1,x_2)$.<br><b>2.</b> Without the absolute value the statement $\\mathrm{d}(x_1,y) - \\mathrm{d}(x_2,y) \\le \\mathrm{d}(x_1,x_2)$ is correct but weaker &ndash; only both directions together control the difference completely."
  },
  "k5_uebung_5_15": {
    title: "Exercise 5.15: Deforming the metric",
    category: "5.2 Metric spaces",
    front: "Show that for a metric space $(X, \\mathrm{d})$ also $\\mathrm{d}_{1/2}(x,y) = \\sqrt{\\mathrm{d}(x,y)}$ and $\\tilde{\\mathrm{d}}(x,y) = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}(x,y)}{1 + \\mathrm{d}(x,y)}$ are metrics on $X$.",
    back: "<b>1. Root metric $\\mathrm{d}_{1/2}$:</b> definiteness and symmetry carry over directly. Triangle inequality: from $\\mathrm{d}(x,z) \\le \\mathrm{d}(x,y) + \\mathrm{d}(y,z)$ and $\\sqrt{a+b} \\le \\sqrt{a} + \\sqrt{b}$ (for $a, b \\ge 0$) follows<br>$$\\sqrt{\\mathrm{d}(x,z)} \\le \\sqrt{\\mathrm{d}(x,y)} + \\sqrt{\\mathrm{d}(y,z)}.$$<br><b>2. Compressed metric $\\tilde{\\mathrm{d}}$:</b> the auxiliary function $f(t) = \\frac{t}{1+t} = 1 - \\frac{1}{1+t}$ is monotonically increasing and subadditive ($f(a+b) \\le f(a) + f(b)$), hence<br>$$\\tilde{\\mathrm{d}}(x,z) = f(\\mathrm{d}(x,z)) \\le f(\\mathrm{d}(x,y) + \\mathrm{d}(y,z)) \\le \\tilde{\\mathrm{d}}(x,y) + \\tilde{\\mathrm{d}}(y,z). \\quad \\square$$",
    meta: "Script: Exercise 5.15, p. 233",
    explanation: "<b>Application:</b> $\\tilde{\\mathrm{d}}$ squeezes any (even unbounded) metric into the interval $[0, 1)$ without changing convergence &ndash; useful when bounded distances are needed. Both constructions show: from one metric many others can be built by composing it with a concave, monotone function $f$ with $f(0)=0$.",
    example: "For $\\mathrm{d}(x,y) = 999$: $\\tilde{\\mathrm{d}}(x,y) = \\frac{999}{1000} = 0{,}999 < 1$. For $\\mathrm{d}(x,y) = \\tfrac14$: $\\mathrm{d}_{1/2}(x,y) = \\tfrac12$ &ndash; small distances are <i>enlarged</i> by the square root.",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> The construction does not work with convex functions: $\\mathrm{d}(x,y)^2$ is in general <i>not</i> a metric (on $\\mathbb{R}$: $\\mathrm{d}(0,2)^2 = 4 > 1 + 1$) &ndash; squaring destroys the triangle inequality, taking roots preserves it.<br><b>2.</b> $\\tilde{\\mathrm{d}}$ and $\\mathrm{d}$ are in general <i>not</i> $C$-equivalent (see the exercise on equivalent metrics): $\\tilde{\\mathrm{d}} < 1$ is bounded, $\\mathrm{d}$ on $\\mathbb{R}$ is not &ndash; same convergence despite the lack of $C$-equivalence."
  },
  "k5_def_5_16": {
    title: "Definition 5.16: Open balls and open sets",
    category: "5.2 Metric spaces",
    front: "How are an <b>open ball $B_r(x_0)$</b> and an <b>open subset $O \\subseteq X$</b> defined in a metric space $(X, \\mathrm{d})$?",
    back: "&bull; For $r > 0$ and $x_0 \\in X$<br>$$B_r(x_0) = \\{x \\in X \\mid \\mathrm{d}(x, x_0) < r\\}$$<br>is the <b>open ball</b> with radius $r$ around $x_0$.<br><br>&bull; A subset $O \\subseteq X$ is <b>open</b> if for every $x_0 \\in O$ there is an $r > 0$ with<br>$$B_r(x_0) \\subseteq O.$$",
    meta: "Script: Definition 5.16, p. 234 (cf. Definition 2.52)",
    explanation: "A set is open if each of its points is an <i>interior</i> point: around every point there is still a small safety ball lying entirely inside the set. Note the <i>strict</i> inequality $\\mathrm{d}(x,x_0) < r$ in the ball definition.<br><br><svg width='100%' height='130' viewBox='0 0 320 130' style='max-width:100%; height:auto; background:#f8fafc; border-radius:8px; margin-top:6px;'><path d='M40,30 Q160,10 280,40 Q300,100 240,110 Q100,120 30,80 Z' fill='#10b981' fill-opacity='0.15' stroke='#10b981' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='140' cy='70' r='30' fill='none' stroke='#1f407a' stroke-dasharray='3 3' stroke-width='1.5'/><circle cx='140' cy='70' r='3' fill='#1f407a'/><text x='140' y='63' font-size='10' text-anchor='middle' font-weight='bold' fill='#1f407a'>x&#8320;</text><text x='175' y='73' font-size='9' fill='#1f407a'>B&#7523;(x&#8320;) &#8838; O</text><text x='250' y='80' font-size='12' font-weight='bold' fill='#10b981'>O</text></svg>",
    example: "Every open interval $(a,b) \\subseteq \\mathbb{R}$ is open with respect to the standard metric: around $x_0 \\in (a,b)$ choose $r = \\min\\{x_0 - a, b - x_0\\} > 0$. Also every open ball itself is open (the proof of Lemma 5.17 shows this).",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> $[a,b]$ is <i>not</i> open in $\\mathbb{R}$: every ball $B_r(a) = (a-r, a+r)$ around the boundary point $a$ contains points outside $[a,b]$.<br><b>2.</b> Openness depends on the surrounding space: $[0, 1)$ is not open in $\\mathbb{R}$, but <i>open in the subspace</i> $X = [0, \\infty)$ with the induced metric, since there $B_1(0) = [0,1)$ &ndash; &ldquo;open&rdquo; is not an absolute property of the set alone."
  },
  "k5_lemma_5_17": {
    title: "Lemma 5.17: Intersections of open balls",
    category: "5.2 Metric spaces",
    front: "What does <b>Lemma 5.17</b> say about the intersection of two open balls, and how is it proved?",
    back: "Let $x_1, x_2 \\in X$ and $r_1, r_2 > 0$. Then $B_{r_1}(x_1) \\cap B_{r_2}(x_2)$ is <b>open</b>: for every $x$ in the intersection there is an $r > 0$ with $B_r(x) \\subseteq B_{r_1}(x_1) \\cap B_{r_2}(x_2)$.<br><br><b>Proof:</b><br>1. Set $r = \\min\\{r_1 - \\mathrm{d}(x, x_1),\\ r_2 - \\mathrm{d}(x, x_2)\\}$; then $r > 0$, since $\\mathrm{d}(x,x_i) < r_i$.<br>2. For $y \\in B_r(x)$ the triangle inequality gives:<br>$$\\mathrm{d}(y, x_1) \\le \\mathrm{d}(y, x) + \\mathrm{d}(x, x_1) < \\big(r_1 - \\mathrm{d}(x,x_1)\\big) + \\mathrm{d}(x,x_1) = r_1$$<br>3. Likewise $\\mathrm{d}(y, x_2) < r_2$, so $B_r(x) \\subseteq B_{r_1}(x_1) \\cap B_{r_2}(x_2)$. $\\square$",
    meta: "Script: Lemma 5.17, pp. 234-235",
    explanation: "<b>Idea:</b> $r$ is the smaller of the two &ldquo;remaining distances to the boundary&rdquo;. This way the new ball stays entirely inside the intersection region.<br><br><svg width='100%' height='135' viewBox='0 0 320 135' style='max-width:100%; height:auto; background:#f8fafc; border-radius:8px; margin-top:6px;'><circle cx='110' cy='68' r='55' fill='none' stroke='#1f407a' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='190' cy='68' r='55' fill='none' stroke='#10b981' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='150' cy='68' r='18' fill='#ef4444' fill-opacity='0.25' stroke='#ef4444' stroke-width='1.5'/><circle cx='150' cy='68' r='2.5' fill='#ef4444'/><text x='150' y='61' text-anchor='middle' font-size='9' font-weight='bold' fill='#ef4444'>x</text><text x='70' y='68' font-size='10' fill='#1f407a'>B&#7523;&#8321;(x&#8321;)</text><text x='205' y='68' font-size='10' fill='#10b981'>B&#7523;&#8322;(x&#8322;)</text></svg>",
    example: "In $\\mathbb{R}$: $B_2(0) \\cap B_2(3) = (-2,2) \\cap (1,5) = (1,2)$ &ndash; again open. For $x = 1{,}5$: $r = \\min\\{2 - 1{,}5,\\ 2 - 1{,}5\\} = 0{,}5$ and indeed $(1, 2) \\supseteq B_{0{,}5}(1{,}5)$.",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> For <i>infinite</i> intersections the statement becomes false: $\\bigcap_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}} B_{1/n}(0) = \\{0\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}$ is not open &ndash; the radii $r$ shrink towards $0$.<br><b>2.</b> The naive radius $r = \\min\\{r_1, r_2\\}$ (instead of the remaining distances) does not work: for $x$ close to the boundary of $B_{r_1}(x_1)$ the ball $B_r(x)$ would otherwise stick out beyond it."
  },
  "k5_uebung_5_18_baelle": {
    title: "Important Exercise 5.18: What do open balls look like?",
    category: "5.2 Metric spaces",
    front: "Describe the open balls $B_r(x_0)$ in the following metric spaces:<br>1. discrete metric, 2. Manhattan metric on $[0,1]^2$, 3. metric $\\mathrm{d}_\\infty$ on $\\mathbb{R}^2$, 4. SNCF metric on $\\mathbb{C}$, 5. $C([0,1])$ with $\\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty$.",
    back: "1. <b>Discrete:</b> $B_r(x_0) = \\{x_0\\}$ for $r \\le 1$ and $B_r(x_0) = X$ for $r > 1$.<br>2. <b>Manhattan:</b> diamonds (squares standing on a tip) $\\{|x - x_0| + |y - y_0| < r\\}$, intersected with $[0,1]^2$.<br>3. <b>$\\mathrm{d}_\\infty$:</b> axis-parallel open squares with side length $2r$ around $x_0$.<br>4. <b>SNCF:</b> For $r \\le |x_0|$: an open segment on the line through the origin and $x_0$ (Paris is not reached). For $r > |x_0|$: this segment <i>plus</i> a star of segments of length $r - |x_0|$ on <i>all</i> half-lines starting at the origin.<br>5. <b>$C([0,1])$, $\\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty$:</b> all continuous functions whose graph runs inside the open &ldquo;$\\varepsilon$-tube&rdquo; of width $2r$ around the graph of $x_0 = f$.",
    meta: "Script: Important Exercise 5.18 & Applet 5.19, p. 235",
    explanation: "<b>Conclusion:</b> the intuition of the &ldquo;round ball&rdquo; is misleading! Depending on the metric, balls are squares, diamonds, single points, stars of segments or function tubes. The <i>shape</i> of the balls encodes the geometry of the metric &ndash; but their role for convergence and openness is always the same.",
    example: "Discrete metric with $r = \\tfrac12$: $B_{1/2}(x_0) = \\{x_0\\}$ &ndash; every singleton is open, so <i>every</i> subset is open (a union of singletons).",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> Balls need not &ldquo;look like circles&rdquo;: the SNCF ball with $r > |x_0|$ is a star of infinitely many segments.<br><b>2.</b> Balls need not even be infinite: in the discrete metric $B_1(x_0) = \\{x_0\\}$ is finite &ndash; the intuition &ldquo;ball = neighbourhood with many points&rdquo; depends on the metric."
  },

  "k5_def_5_20": {
    title: "Definition 5.20: Sequences",
    category: "5.3 Sequences and convergence",
    front: "What is formally a <b>sequence</b> in a set $X$, and when is it <b>constant</b> or <b>eventually constant</b>?",
    back: "A <b>sequence</b> in $X$ is a map $a: \\mathbb{N} \\to X$. One writes $a_n = a(n)$ (the $n$-th <b>term</b>) and denotes the sequence by $(a_1, a_2, \\dots)$, $(a_n)_{n \\in \\mathbb{N}}$, $(a_n)_{n=1}^\\infty$ or briefly $(a_n)_n$. The set of all sequences in $X$ is $X^{\\mathbb{N}}$.<br><br>&bull; <b>constant:</b> $a_n = a_m$ for all $m, n \\in \\mathbb{N}$<br>&bull; <b>eventually constant:</b> there exists $N \\in \\mathbb{N}$ with $a_n = a_m$ for all $m, n \\ge N$",
    meta: "Script: Definition 5.20, p. 236",
    explanation: "A sequence is an infinite, ordered list. &ldquo;Eventually constant&rdquo; means: from some index on it freezes at a fixed value &ndash; the simplest kind of convergent behaviour and the starting point for the general definition of convergence.<br><br>If $X = V$ is a vector space, then the sequences with $(a_n)_n + (b_n)_n = (a_n + b_n)_n$ and $\\alpha (a_n)_n = (\\alpha a_n)_n$ themselves form a vector space.",
    example: "$a_n = 5$ for $n \\ge 10$ and $a_n = n$ for $n < 10$ is eventually constant (from $N = 10$). The sequence $(\\tfrac1n)_n = (1, \\tfrac12, \\tfrac13, \\dots)$ is neither constant nor eventually constant &ndash; but it is convergent.",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> $a_n = (-1)^n$ is not eventually constant: it switches infinitely often between $-1$ and $1$.<br><b>2.</b> A sequence is more than its set of values: $(1, 2, 1, 2, \\dots)$ and $(1, 1, 2, 2, 1, 1, \\dots)$ have the same set of values $\\{1,2\\}$ but are different sequences &ndash; the map $n \\mapsto a_n$ counts, not just the image."
  },
  "k5_def_5_21": {
    title: "Definition 5.21: Convergence in metric spaces",
    category: "5.3 Sequences and convergence",
    front: "How is the <b>convergence</b> of a sequence $(a_n)_n$ in a metric space $(X, \\mathrm{d})$ defined? What do <b>convergent</b> and <b>divergent</b> mean?",
    back: "$(a_n)_n$ <b>converges</b> (or <b>tends</b>) to $A \\in X$ if for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is $N \\in \\mathbb{N}$ such that $\\mathrm{d}(a_n, A) < \\varepsilon$ for all $n \\ge N$. In predicate logic:<br>$$\\forall \\varepsilon > 0\\ \\exists N \\in \\mathbb{N}\\ \\forall n \\ge N: \\mathrm{d}(a_n, A) < \\varepsilon$$<br>$A$ is then called the <b>limit</b>, written $\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} a_n = A$.<br><br>A sequence is called <b>convergent</b> if it has a limit, and <b>divergent</b> otherwise.<br><br><i>Equivalently:</i> $(a_n)_n \\to A$ in $X$ $\\iff$ the real sequence $(\\mathrm{d}(a_n, A))_n$ converges to $0$.",
    meta: "Script: Definition 5.21, pp. 236-237",
    explanation: "<b>$\\varepsilon$-ball intuition:</b> no matter how small the ball $B_\\varepsilon(A)$ is chosen &ndash; from some index $N$ on, <i>all further</i> terms lie inside it. It even suffices to check the condition for all small $\\varepsilon \\in (0, \\varepsilon_0)$ (Exercise 5.23).<br><br><svg width='100%' height='130' viewBox='0 0 320 130' style='max-width:100%; height:auto; background:#f8fafc; border-radius:8px;'><circle cx='210' cy='65' r='45' fill='#10b981' fill-opacity='0.15' stroke='#10b981' stroke-dasharray='3 3' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='210' cy='65' r='3' fill='#1f407a'/><text x='210' y='55' text-anchor='middle' font-weight='bold' fill='#1f407a'>A</text><circle cx='30' cy='100' r='3' fill='#ef4444'/><circle cx='75' cy='30' r='3' fill='#ef4444'/><circle cx='130' cy='90' r='3' fill='#ef4444'/><circle cx='185' cy='75' r='3' fill='#10b981'/><circle cx='220' cy='60' r='3' fill='#10b981'/><circle cx='205' cy='68' r='3' fill='#10b981'/><text x='30' y='115' font-size='9' fill='#666'>a&#8321;</text><text x='185' y='90' font-size='9' font-weight='bold' fill='#10b981'>a&#8342;</text></svg>",
    example: "In $(\\mathbb{R}, |\\cdot|)$: $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\tfrac1n = 0$, because for $\\varepsilon > 0$ the Archimedean principle (Theorem 2.68) gives an $N$ with $\\tfrac1N < \\varepsilon$, and for $n \\ge N$ we have $|\\tfrac1n - 0| \\le \\tfrac1N < \\varepsilon$ (Example 5.28).",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> $a_n = (-1)^n$ is divergent: for every $A$, infinitely many terms lie at distance $\\ge 1$ from $A$ (Example 5.28).<br><b>2.</b> Convergence depends on the metric: $a_n = \\tfrac1n$ converges to $0$ in $(\\mathbb{R}, |\\cdot|)$, but with respect to the <i>discrete</i> metric the same sequence diverges, since $\\mathrm{d}_{\\text{discrete}}(a_n, 0) = 1$ for all $n$."
  },
  "k5_lemma_5_22": {
    title: "Lemma 5.22: Uniqueness of the limit",
    category: "5.3 Sequences and convergence",
    front: "Show that every convergent sequence in a metric space has a <b>unique</b> limit.",
    back: "<b>Proof (by contradiction):</b><br>1. Suppose $A_1 \\neq A_2$ are two limits of $(a_n)_n$. Then $\\varepsilon = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}(A_1, A_2)}{2} > 0$.<br>2. Convergence to $A_1$: $\\exists N_1$ with $\\mathrm{d}(a_n, A_1) < \\varepsilon$ for $n \\ge N_1$; to $A_2$: $\\exists N_2$ analogously.<br>3. For $N = \\max\\{N_1, N_2\\}$ the triangle inequality gives:<br>$$\\mathrm{d}(A_1, A_2) \\le \\mathrm{d}(A_1, a_N) + \\mathrm{d}(a_N, A_2) < 2\\varepsilon = \\mathrm{d}(A_1, A_2),$$<br>a contradiction. $\\square$<br><br>That is why one speaks of <b>the</b> limit $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} a_n$.",
    meta: "Script: Lemma 5.22 & Exercise 5.23, p. 237",
    explanation: "<b>Idea:</b> two points at positive distance can be separated by disjoint $\\varepsilon$-balls &ndash; but a sequence cannot eventually stay in both balls at the same time.<br><br><svg width='100%' height='110' viewBox='0 0 320 110' style='max-width:100%; height:auto; background:#f8fafc; border-radius:8px;'><circle cx='90' cy='55' r='35' fill='none' stroke='#1f407a' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='230' cy='55' r='35' fill='none' stroke='#ef4444' stroke-width='2'/><circle cx='90' cy='55' r='3' fill='#1f407a'/><circle cx='230' cy='55' r='3' fill='#ef4444'/><text x='90' y='50' text-anchor='middle' font-size='10' font-weight='bold' fill='#1f407a'>A&#8321;</text><text x='230' y='50' text-anchor='middle' font-size='10' font-weight='bold' fill='#ef4444'>A&#8322;</text><line x1='90' y1='55' x2='230' y2='55' stroke='#666' stroke-dasharray='2 2'/><text x='160' y='45' text-anchor='middle' font-size='9' fill='#666'>d(A&#8321;, A&#8322;)</text></svg>",
    example: "The choice $\\varepsilon = \\frac{\\mathrm{d}(A_1,A_2)}{2}$ is the standard trick: half the distance guarantees disjoint balls $B_\\varepsilon(A_1) \\cap B_\\varepsilon(A_2) = \\emptyset$.",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> Uniqueness uses the <i>definiteness</i> of the metric: with a &ldquo;pseudometric&rdquo; such as $\\mathrm{d}(x,y) = |x^2 - y^2|$ on $\\mathbb{R}$, the constant sequence $a_n = 1$ would have the two &ldquo;limits&rdquo; $1$ and $-1$.<br><b>2.</b> <i>Cluster points</i> are not unique: $(-1)^n$ has the two cluster points $\\pm 1$ &ndash; uniqueness is a special property of the limit, not every &ldquo;approach concept&rdquo; has it."
  },
  "k5_def_5_24": {
    title: "Definition 5.24: Neighbourhoods & Lemma 5.25: Index shift",
    category: "5.3 Sequences and convergence",
    front: "What is a <b>neighbourhood</b> of $x_0 \\in X$? How does one characterise convergence via neighbourhoods, and what does the <b>index shift</b> (Lemma 5.25) say?",
    back: "&bull; <b>Neighbourhood (Def. 5.24):</b> a subset $U \\subseteq X$ is a neighbourhood of $x_0$ if it contains an $\\varepsilon$-ball $B_\\varepsilon(x_0)$ for some $\\varepsilon > 0$.<br><br>&bull; <b>Neighbourhood characterisation:</b> $(x_n)_n \\to x_0$ if and only if for <i>every</i> neighbourhood $U$ of $x_0$ <b>almost all</b> (= all except finitely many) terms of the sequence lie in $U$.<br><br>&bull; <b>Lemma 5.25 (index shift):</b> for $\\ell \\in \\mathbb{N}_0$, $(a_n)_n$ is convergent if and only if $(a_{n+\\ell})_n$ is convergent; in this case $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} a_n = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty} a_{n+\\ell}$.",
    meta: "Script: Definition 5.24, Lemma 5.25, Exercise 5.26, p. 238",
    explanation: "The neighbourhood formulation makes convergence independent of concrete $\\varepsilon$ values and paves the way to topology (2nd semester). &ldquo;Almost all&rdquo; is the key notion: finitely many outliers at the beginning are always allowed &ndash; exactly what the index shift formalises: convergence is a property of the tail of the sequence.",
    example: "$[0, 2]$ is a neighbourhood of $1$ in $\\mathbb{R}$ (it contains $B_{1/2}(1)$), although $[0,2]$ is not open. Index shift: $\\lim_n \\frac{1}{n+5} = \\lim_n \\frac1n = 0$.",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> $[1, 2]$ is <i>not</i> a neighbourhood of $1$: no ball $B_\\varepsilon(1) = (1-\\varepsilon, 1+\\varepsilon)$ fits inside it.<br><b>2.</b> &ldquo;Infinitely many terms in every neighbourhood&rdquo; is <i>not</i> enough for convergence: $(-1)^n$ has infinitely many terms in every neighbourhood of $1$, yet does not converge &ndash; one needs <i>almost all</i> terms (this is the difference between a cluster point and a limit)."
  },
  "k5_lemma_5_27": {
    title: "Lemma 5.27: Convergent sequences are bounded",
    category: "5.3 Sequences and convergence",
    front: "When is a sequence in a normed vector space <b>bounded</b>, and why is every convergent sequence bounded (Lemma 5.27)?",
    back: "A sequence $(a_n)_n$ in $(V, \\|\\cdot\\|)$ is called <b>bounded</b> if there is $M > 0$ with $\\|a_n\\| \\le M$ for all $n \\in \\mathbb{N}$.<br><br><b>Lemma 5.27:</b> Every convergent sequence in $V$ is bounded.<br><br><b>Proof:</b><br>1. Let $A = \\lim a_n$. For $\\varepsilon = 1$ there is $N$ with $\\|a_n - A\\| < 1$ for $n \\ge N$.<br>2. Triangle inequality: $\\|a_n\\| = \\|a_n - A + A\\| \\le \\|a_n - A\\| + \\|A\\| < 1 + \\|A\\|$ for $n \\ge N$.<br>3. Altogether: $\\|a_n\\| \\le \\max\\{\\|a_1\\|, \\dots, \\|a_{N-1}\\|,\\ 1 + \\|A\\|\\}$ for all $n$. $\\square$",
    meta: "Script: Lemma 5.27, p. 238 (generalisation: exercise in 5.5.2)",
    explanation: "<b>Principle:</b> from index $N$ on, all terms sit in the ball $B_1(A)$ &ndash; which is bounded. The finitely many terms before it can only enlarge the bound by a maximum of finitely many values. &ldquo;Finitely many exceptions never harm boundedness.&rdquo; The contrapositive is an important divergence criterion: <b>unbounded $\\Rightarrow$ divergent</b>.",
    example: "$a_n = \\tfrac1n$: convergent to $0$ and bounded by $M = 1$. Applying the contrapositive: $q^n$ with $|q| > 1$ is unbounded (Bernoulli inequality), hence divergent (Example 5.34 (iv)).",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> The converse is false: $a_n = (-1)^n$ is bounded ($M = 1$), but divergent.<br><b>2.</b> Choosing $\\varepsilon = 1$ fixed is allowed here, because boundedness only needs <i>some</i> bound &ndash; but beware: for a <i>convergence</i> proof one must never fix $\\varepsilon$; there the condition has to hold for <i>all</i> $\\varepsilon > 0$."
  },
  "k5_bsp_5_28": {
    title: "Example 5.28 & Exercise 5.29: First convergent and divergent sequences",
    category: "5.3 Sequences and convergence",
    front: "Discuss the basic examples from the script: constant sequences, $\\left(\\tfrac1n\\right)_n$ and $\\left((-1)^n\\right)_n$. What does the <b>Important Exercise 5.29</b> say?",
    back: "&bull; <b>Constant sequence</b> $a_n = A$: converges to $A$; likewise eventually constant sequences converge to their final value.<br><br>&bull; $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} \\tfrac1n = 0$: for $\\varepsilon > 0$ the <b>Archimedean principle</b> (Theorem 2.68) gives an $N$ with $\\tfrac1N < \\varepsilon$; for $n \\ge N$ it follows that $0 \\le \\tfrac1n \\le \\tfrac1N < \\varepsilon$.<br><br>&bull; $a_n = (-1)^n$ is <b>divergent</b>: for $A \\neq 1$ set $\\varepsilon = |A - 1| > 0$; there are arbitrarily late even $n$ with $|a_n - A| = |1 - A| = \\varepsilon \\not< \\varepsilon$. For $A = 1$ the odd $n$ give the distance $|1 - (-1)| = 2$.<br><br>&bull; <b>Important Exercise 5.29:</b> If $(a_n)_n$ is a convergent sequence in $\\mathbb{C}$ with $a_n \\in \\mathbb{R}$ for all $n$, then $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} a_n$ is also real.",
    meta: "Script: Example 5.28, Important Exercise 5.29, p. 239",
    explanation: "These three examples are the prototypes: <i>standing still</i>, <i>falling towards a point</i>, <i>oscillating forever</i>. The proof for $\\tfrac1n$ shows the typical pattern of every $\\varepsilon$-$N$ proof: $\\varepsilon$ given, construct $N$, estimate for all $n \\ge N$. The divergence of $(-1)^n$ shows the pattern of a negative proof: exclude <i>every</i> candidate $A$.<br><br>A convergent sequence with limit $0$ is called a <b>null sequence</b>.",
    example: "With the arithmetic rules (Prop. 5.30) more follows immediately: $\\frac{7n^4 + 15}{3n^4 + n^3 + n - 1} \\to \\frac73$ (divide by $n^4$; Exercise 5.32).",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> $(-1)^n$: bounded, but divergent &ndash; boundedness is not enough for convergence.<br><b>2.</b> Exercise 5.33: if $(a_n)_n$ is complex-valued with $a_n \\neq 0$ and $(a_n^{-1})_n \\to 0$, then $(a_n)_n$ is <i>divergent</i> (it is unbounded) &ndash; &ldquo;the reciprocal becomes small&rdquo; does not mean that the sequence itself converges."
  },
  "k5_prop_5_30": {
    title: "Proposition 5.30: Arithmetic rules for limits",
    category: "5.3 Sequences and convergence",
    front: "Which <b>additive and multiplicative properties</b> does the limit of convergent sequences $(a_n)_n, (b_n)_n$ in $\\mathbb{C}$ have (Proposition 5.30)?",
    back: "Let $\\lim a_n = A$ and $\\lim b_n = B$. Then:<br><br>(i) <b>Sum:</b> $(a_n + b_n)_n$ converges with $\\lim (a_n + b_n) = A + B$.<br>(ii) <b>Product:</b> $(a_n b_n)_n$ converges with $\\lim (a_n b_n) = A \\cdot B$; in particular $\\lim (\\alpha a_n) = \\alpha A$ for $\\alpha \\in \\mathbb{C}$.<br>(iii) <b>Reciprocal:</b> if $a_n \\neq 0$ for all $n$ and $A \\neq 0$, then $\\left(\\tfrac{1}{a_n}\\right)_n$ converges with $\\lim \\tfrac{1}{a_n} = \\tfrac{1}{A}$.<br><br><i>Consequence:</i> the convergent sequences form a subspace of $\\mathbb{C}^{\\mathbb{N}}$, and $\\lim$ is a linear map to $\\mathbb{C}$.",
    meta: "Script: Proposition 5.30, pp. 239-241",
    explanation: "<b>Proof tricks:</b> for the sum: $|(a_n + b_n) - (A+B)| \\le |a_n - A| + |b_n - B| < \\tfrac\\varepsilon2 + \\tfrac\\varepsilon2$. For the product the <b>mixed-term decomposition</b>:<br>$$|a_nb_n - AB| \\le |a_n - A||b_n| + |A||b_n - B|,$$<br>where $|b_n| \\le 1 + |B|$ for large $n$ (boundedness, Lemma 5.27). For the reciprocal the reverse triangle inequality prevents $a_n$ from becoming too small: $|a_n| > \\tfrac{|A|}{2}$ for large $n$.",
    example: "$$\\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{3n + 1}{2n - 5} = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\frac{3 + \\tfrac1n}{2 - \\tfrac5n} = \\frac{3 + 0}{2 - 0} = \\frac{3}{2}$$ &ndash; divide numerator and denominator by $n$ and apply (i)&ndash;(iii) (cf. Exercise 5.32).",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> The converse of (i) is false: $a_n = (-1)^n$ and $b_n = (-1)^{n+1}$ both diverge, but their sum $a_n + b_n = 0$ converges.<br><b>2.</b> In (iii) $A \\neq 0$ is essential: for $a_n = \\tfrac1n \\to 0$ the reciprocal sequence $\\tfrac{1}{a_n} = n$ diverges (unbounded). Also $a_n \\neq 0$ for all $n$ is needed for the reciprocal sequence to be defined at all."
  },
  "k5_bsp_5_34": {
    title: "Example 5.34: Geometric sequences",
    category: "5.3 Sequences and convergence",
    front: "Investigate the convergence behaviour of the <b>geometric sequence</b> $(q^n)_n$ for $q \\in \\mathbb{C}$ in all cases.",
    back: "&bull; <b>$q = 1$:</b> $q^n = 1 \\to 1$.<br>&bull; <b>$|q| < 1$:</b> $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} q^n = 0$ (null sequence).<br>&bull; <b>$|q| > 1$:</b> $(q^n)_n$ is unbounded and therefore divergent.<br>&bull; <b>$|q| = 1$, $q \\neq 1$:</b> bounded, but divergent (e.g. $q = -1$).",
    meta: "Script: Example 5.34 & Exercise 5.35, pp. 241-242",
    explanation: "<b>Proof ideas from the script:</b><br>&bull; $|q| > 1$: write $|q| = 1 + x$ with $x > 0$. Bernoulli (Lemma 3.5) + Archimedes: $|q|^N \\ge 1 + Nx > M$ &ndash; unbounded, hence divergent by Lemma 5.27.<br>&bull; $|q| < 1$, $q \\neq 0$: $|q^{-1}| > 1$, so there is $N$ with $|q|^{-N} > \\tfrac1\\varepsilon$, i.e. $|q^n| \\le |q|^N < \\varepsilon$ for $n \\ge N$.<br>&bull; $|q| = 1$, $q \\neq 1$ (elegant!): if $\\lim q^n = A$, then $A = \\lim q^{n+1} = q \\lim q^n = qA$ (Prop. 5.30 + index shift Lemma 5.25), so $(q-1)A = 0$ and $A = 0$ &ndash; contradicting $|q^n| = 1$ for all $n$.",
    example: "$q = 0{,}5$: $\\lim (0{,}5)^n = 0$. Even $nq^n \\to 0$ for $|q| < 1$ (Exercise 5.35) &ndash; geometric decay beats linear growth.",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> $q = -1$: the sequence $(-1, 1, -1, \\dots)$ is bounded but has no limit.<br><b>2.</b> $q = i$ (with $|q| = 1$): $(i, -1, -i, 1, i, \\dots)$ cycles periodically through four values on the unit circle &ndash; bounded, divergent; so the case $|q| = 1, q \\neq 1$ is genuinely divergent even for complex $q$."
  },
  "k5_uebung_5_36": {
    title: "Exercise 5.36: Cesàro means",
    category: "5.3 Sequences and convergence",
    front: "What does Exercise 5.36 say about the <b>Cesàro means</b> (arithmetic means) $b_n = \\frac{1}{n}\\sum_{k=1}^n a_k$ of a convergent sequence?",
    back: "Let $(a_n)_n$ be a convergent sequence in $\\mathbb{C}$. Then the sequence of <b>Cesàro means</b> (also called <b>arithmetic means</b> or <b>Cauchy means</b>)<br>$$b_n = \\frac{1}{n} \\sum_{k=1}^n a_k$$<br>also converges and has the <b>same limit</b> as $(a_n)_n$.<br><br><b>The converse does not hold:</b> convergence of the Cesàro means does not imply convergence of the sequence itself.",
    meta: "Script: Exercise 5.36, p. 242",
    explanation: "<b>Proof idea:</b> split the sum into an &ldquo;early&rdquo; part (finitely many terms, whose contribution $\\tfrac{1}{n}\\sum_{k \\le N}|a_k - A|$ vanishes as $n$ grows) and a &ldquo;late&rdquo; part in which all $a_k$ already lie $\\varepsilon$-close to $A$. Averaging thus <i>smooths</i>: it preserves limits but irons out oscillations &ndash; the entry point to the theory of summability of divergent sequences.",
    example: "$a_n = \\tfrac1n \\to 0$: also $b_n = \\tfrac1n\\left(1 + \\tfrac12 + \\dots + \\tfrac1n\\right) \\to 0$ (the harmonic partial sums grow more slowly than $n$).",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> $a_n = (-1)^n$ diverges, but the Cesàro means $b_n \\in \\{0, -\\tfrac1n\\}$ converge to $0$ &ndash; Cesàro convergence is genuinely weaker than convergence.<br><b>2.</b> Cesàro does not rescue everything: for the unbounded sequence $a_n = n$ the means $b_n = \\frac{n+1}{2}$ diverge too &ndash; averaging helps only against oscillation, not against growth."
  },
  "k5_def_5_38": {
    title: "Definition 5.38 & Lemma 5.39: Subsequences",
    category: "5.3 Sequences and convergence",
    front: "What is a <b>subsequence</b>, and what does <b>Lemma 5.39</b> say about subsequences of convergent sequences?",
    back: "&bull; <b>Definition 5.38:</b> if $(a_n)_n$ is a sequence in $X$ and $(n_k)_k: k \\in \\mathbb{N} \\mapsto n_k \\in \\mathbb{N}$ is a <i>strictly increasing</i> sequence, then $(a_{n_k})_k$ is called a <b>subsequence</b> of $(a_n)_n$.<br><br>&bull; <b>Lemma 5.39:</b> if $(a_n)_n$ converges in a metric space $(X,\\mathrm{d})$, then <i>every</i> subsequence $(a_{n_k})_k$ converges and has the same limit: $\\lim_{k\\to\\infty} a_{n_k} = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty} a_n$ (proof: Important Exercise 5.40).",
    meta: "Script: Definition 5.38, Lemma 5.39, Exercises 5.40/5.41, pp. 242-243",
    explanation: "<b>Basis of the proof:</b> strict monotonicity implies $n_k \\ge k$ &ndash; the subsequence runs ahead of the main sequence and inherits every $\\varepsilon$-$N$ condition.<br><br><b>Divergence tool:</b> two subsequences with <i>different</i> limits $\\Rightarrow$ the main sequence diverges (an elegant short proof for $(-1)^n$!).<br><br><b>Exercise 5.41:</b> conversely, $(a_n)_n$ converges to $A$ exactly when <i>every</i> subsequence has a further subsequence converging to $A$.",
    example: "$a_n = \\tfrac1n$: the subsequence of even indices $a_{2k} = \\tfrac{1}{2k}$ also converges to $0$. The sequence $(n_k)_k$ must be strictly increasing: $n_k = 2k$ is allowed.",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> $a_n = (-1)^n$: $a_{2k} = 1 \\to 1$ and $a_{2k+1} = -1 \\to -1$ &ndash; two subsequence limits, so the main sequence diverges.<br><b>2.</b> Convergent subsequences alone do not suffice: $(0, 1, 0, 2, 0, 3, \\dots)$ has the convergent subsequence $(0,0,0,\\dots)$, yet is divergent (even unbounded)."
  },
  "k5_prop_5_42": {
    title: "Proposition 5.42: Cluster points of a sequence",
    category: "5.3 Sequences and convergence",
    front: "How is a <b>cluster point</b> of a sequence defined, and which two conditions are equivalent (Proposition 5.42)?",
    back: "Let $(a_n)_n$ be a sequence in the metric space $(X, \\mathrm{d})$. A point $A \\in X$ is called a <b>cluster point</b> of $(a_n)_n$ if the following <i>equivalent</i> conditions hold:<br><br>(a) There is a subsequence $(a_{n_k})_k$ with $\\lim_{k \\to \\infty} a_{n_k} = A$.<br><br>(b) For all $\\varepsilon > 0$ and all $N \\in \\mathbb{N}$ there is $n \\ge N$ with $\\mathrm{d}(a_n, A) < \\varepsilon$<br>(i.e. every $\\varepsilon$-ball around $A$ contains infinitely many terms of the sequence).",
    meta: "Script: Proposition 5.42, pp. 243-244",
    explanation: "<b>Limit vs. cluster point:</b><br>&bull; <i>Limit:</i> <b>almost all</b> terms in every $\\varepsilon$-ball.<br>&bull; <i>Cluster point:</i> <b>infinitely many</b> terms in every $\\varepsilon$-ball (&ldquo;keeps coming back&rdquo;).<br><br><b>Proof (b) $\\Rightarrow$ (a):</b> recursively choose $n_1 < n_2 < \\dots$ with $\\mathrm{d}(a_{n_k}, A) < \\tfrac1k$. The script notes that a (weak form of the) <b>axiom of choice</b> enters here &ndash; avoidable by always choosing the minimal $n_{k+1}$.",
    example: "$a_n = (-1)^n$ has exactly the cluster points $1$ and $-1$. Convergent sequences have exactly one cluster point: their limit.",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> A single cluster point does not guarantee convergence: $(0, 1, 0, 2, 0, 3, \\dots)$ has only the cluster point $0$, yet diverges (unbounded).<br><b>2.</b> Do not confuse cluster points of a <i>sequence</i> with cluster points of a <i>set</i> (Def. 2.73): the constant sequence $(5, 5, 5, \\dots)$ has the sequence cluster point $5$, but its set of values $\\{5\\}$ has no set cluster point &ndash; that would require other points arbitrarily close."
  },
  "k5_prop_5_44": {
    title: "Proposition 5.44: Convergence in Cᵈ and Rᵈ",
    category: "5.3 Sequences and convergence",
    front: "What does <b>Proposition 5.44</b> say about convergence with respect to $\\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty$, $\\|\\cdot\\|_1$, $\\|\\cdot\\|_2$ and componentwise convergence in $\\mathbb{C}^d$?",
    back: "For a sequence $(v_n)_n$ in $\\mathbb{C}^d$ and $v \\in \\mathbb{C}^d$ the following are equivalent:<br><br>(i) $(v_n)_n \\to v$ with respect to $\\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty$<br>(ii) $(v_n)_n \\to v$ with respect to $\\|\\cdot\\|_1$<br>(iii) $(v_n)_n \\to v$ with respect to $\\|\\cdot\\|_2$<br>(iv) For all $j = 1,\\dots,d$ the sequence $(\\pi_j(v_n))_n$ converges to $\\pi_j(v)$ (<b>componentwise convergence</b>).<br><br>The same holds for sequences in $\\mathbb{R}^d$.<br><br><b>Basis of the proof</b> are the norm comparisons (5.2), (5.3):<br>$$\\|w\\|_\\infty \\le \\|w\\|_1 \\le d\\,\\|w\\|_\\infty, \\qquad \\|w\\|_\\infty \\le \\|w\\|_2 \\le \\sqrt{d}\\,\\|w\\|_\\infty$$",
    meta: "Script: Proposition 5.44, pp. 244-245",
    explanation: "In finite-dimensional spaces the notion of convergence does <i>not</i> depend on the choice of these norms (later even: on no norm at all) &ndash; that is why one simply speaks of &ldquo;convergence in $\\mathbb{C}^d$&rdquo;. Convergence in $d$ dimensions = $d$ separate convergences in $\\mathbb{C}$.<br><br>The inequalities are easy to see: the maximum of the absolute values is at most their sum, and the sum is at most $d$ times the maximum.",
    example: "In $\\mathbb{R}^2$: $v_n = \\left(\\tfrac1n,\\ 2 + \\tfrac{1}{n^2}\\right)^t \\to (0, 2)^t$, since both components converge. <b>Corollary 5.45:</b> a complex sequence converges exactly when real and imaginary parts converge (identify $\\mathbb{C} \\cong \\mathbb{R}^2$).",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> For <i>general</i> sets the metric-independence is false: on $[0,1]^2$ there are sequences that converge with respect to the Manhattan metric but not with respect to the SNCF metric (Exercise 5.43) &ndash; e.g. points that approach the target from a different line through the origin.<br><b>2.</b> In <i>infinite-dimensional</i> spaces the equivalence also fails: on $C([a,b])$ the norms $\\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty$ and $\\|\\cdot\\|_1$ define different convergence notions (Exercise 5.47)."
  },
  "k5_kor_5_45": {
    title: "Corollary 5.45 & Exercise 5.46: Reduction to real and imaginary parts",
    category: "5.3 Sequences and convergence",
    front: "What does <b>Corollary 5.45 (reduction)</b> say about complex sequences, and what holds for the absolute values $(|a_n|)_n$ (Exercise 5.46)?",
    back: "<b>Corollary 5.45:</b> a complex-valued sequence $(a_n)_n$ is convergent (with limit $a \\in \\mathbb{C}$) if and only if the two real-valued sequences $(\\operatorname{Re}(a_n))_n$ and $(\\operatorname{Im}(a_n))_n$ converge (with limits $\\operatorname{Re}(a)$ and $\\operatorname{Im}(a)$ respectively).<br><br><b>Proof:</b> identify $\\mathbb{C}$ with $\\mathbb{R}^2$ via $\\phi: x \\mapsto (\\operatorname{Re}(x), \\operatorname{Im}(x))^t$; then $|x - y| = \\|\\phi(x) - \\phi(y)\\|_2$, and the claim follows from Proposition 5.44 (iv). $\\square$<br><br><b>Exercise 5.46:</b> if $(a_n)_n$ converges to $a$ in $\\mathbb{C}$, then $(|a_n|)_n$ converges to $|a|$.",
    meta: "Script: Corollary 5.45 & Exercise 5.46, pp. 245-246",
    explanation: "Complex convergence is therefore nothing new: two real convergences at the same time. For the statement about absolute values the reverse triangle inequality takes care of it: $||a_n| - |a|| \\le |a_n - a|$ &ndash; the absolute value is 1-Lipschitz and hence &ldquo;preserves convergence&rdquo;.",
    example: "$a_n = \\tfrac1n + i\\left(2 - \\tfrac1{n^2}\\right)$: $\\operatorname{Re}(a_n) \\to 0$, $\\operatorname{Im}(a_n) \\to 2$, so $a_n \\to 2i$ and $|a_n| \\to |2i| = 2$.",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> The converse of Exercise 5.46 is false: $(|a_n|)_n$ can converge without $(a_n)_n$ converging &ndash; $a_n = (-1)^n$ gives $|a_n| = 1 \\to 1$, but $(a_n)_n$ diverges.<br><b>2.</b> Only one convergent component is not enough: $a_n = (-1)^n + \\tfrac{i}{n}$ has a convergent imaginary part and a divergent real part &ndash; the sequence diverges; <i>both</i> components are really needed."
  },
  "k5_uebung_5_47": {
    title: "Exercise 5.47: Uniform convergence vs. convergence in the mean",
    category: "5.3 Sequences and convergence",
    front: "Compare, on $V = C([a,b])$, convergence with respect to $\\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty$ and with respect to $\\|\\cdot\\|_1$ (Exercise 5.47). Which implication holds, which does not?",
    back: "(i) <b>$\\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty$-convergence $\\Rightarrow$ $\\|\\cdot\\|_1$-convergence:</b> from $f_n \\to f$ with respect to $\\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty$ it follows that $f_n \\to f$ with respect to $\\|\\cdot\\|_1$, because<br>$$\\|f_n - f\\|_1 = \\int_a^b |f_n - f|\\,\\mathrm{d}x \\le (b-a)\\,\\|f_n - f\\|_\\infty.$$<br>(ii) <b>The converse is false:</b> there are sequences $(f_n)_n$ in $V$ with $\\|f_n\\|_1 \\to 0$, but $\\|f_n\\|_\\infty = 1$ for all $n$.<br><br><b>Terminology:</b> $\\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty$-convergence is called <b>uniform convergence</b>, $\\|\\cdot\\|_1$-convergence is called <b>convergence in the mean</b> &ndash; two genuinely different notions.",
    meta: "Script: Exercise 5.47, p. 246",
    explanation: "<b>Why (ii) works:</b> build ever narrower &ldquo;spikes&rdquo;: let $f_n$ be a triangle of height $1$ over an interval of width $\\tfrac2n$ (and $0$ elsewhere). Then $\\|f_n\\|_\\infty = 1$ is constant, but the area $\\|f_n\\|_1 = \\tfrac1n \\to 0$. The maximal deviation stays, the area vanishes. This shows the fundamental difference between finite- and infinite-dimensional spaces: in $\\mathbb{C}^d$ such a thing would be impossible (Prop. 5.44).",
    example: "A concrete positive direction: $f_n(x) = \\tfrac1n \\sin(nx)$ on $[0, 2\\pi]$ converges uniformly to $0$ ($\\|f_n\\|_\\infty = \\tfrac1n$), hence also in the mean.",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> The spike sequence: $\\|f_n\\|_1 = \\tfrac1n \\to 0$, but $\\|f_n\\|_\\infty = 1$ &ndash; convergence in the mean without uniform convergence.<br><b>2.</b> Even less does pointwise $\\not\\Rightarrow$ uniform: $f_n$ as above with a moving spike converges to $0$ for every fixed $x$, but not uniformly &ndash; a foretaste of the convergence notions for function sequences later in the lecture."
  },

  "k5_def_5_48": {
    title: "Definition 5.48: ε-δ continuity and sequential continuity",
    category: "5.4 Continuity",
    front: "Define <b>$\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ continuity</b> and <b>sequential continuity</b> of a function $f: X \\to Y$ between metric spaces at a point $x_0 \\in X$.",
    back: "Let $(X, \\mathrm{d}_X)$, $(Y, \\mathrm{d}_Y)$ be metric spaces and $f: X \\to Y$.<br><br>&bull; $f$ is <b>$\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ continuous at $x_0 \\in X$</b> if for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is a $\\delta > 0$ such that for all $x \\in B_\\delta(x_0)$ also $f(x) \\in B_\\varepsilon(f(x_0))$.<br><br>&bull; $f$ is <b>sequentially continuous at $x_0$</b> if for every convergent sequence $(x_n)_n$ in $X$ with $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} x_n = x_0$ the image sequence $(f(x_n))_n$ converges with $\\lim_{n\\to\\infty} f(x_n) = f(x_0)$.",
    meta: "Script: Definition 5.48, p. 247",
    explanation: "<b>Key message of sequential continuity:</b> limit and function may be interchanged: $\\lim f(x_n) = f(\\lim x_n)$. The $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ version generalises Definition 3.45 word for word from $\\mathbb{R}$ to metric spaces &ndash; absolute values become metrics, intervals become balls.<br><br><svg width='100%' height='130' viewBox='0 0 320 130' style='max-width:100%; height:auto; background:#f8fafc; border-radius:8px;'><line x1='30' y1='100' x2='130' y2='100' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='1.5'/><circle cx='80' cy='100' r='3' fill='#1f407a'/><line x1='50' y1='100' x2='110' y2='100' stroke='#10b981' stroke-width='4'/><text x='80' y='118' text-anchor='middle' font-size='10' font-weight='bold' fill='#1f407a'>x&#8320; (&#177;&#948;)</text><path d='M130,70 Q165,40 195,60' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='1.5' fill='none'/><line x1='230' y1='100' x2='230' y2='20' stroke='#94a3b8' stroke-width='1.5'/><circle cx='230' cy='60' r='3' fill='#1f407a'/><line x1='230' y1='40' x2='230' y2='80' stroke='#ef4444' stroke-width='4'/><text x='268' y='64' text-anchor='middle' font-size='10' font-weight='bold' fill='#ef4444'>f(x&#8320;) (&#177;&#949;)</text></svg>",
    example: "The distance function $x \\mapsto \\mathrm{d}(x, y_0)$ to a fixed point $y_0$ is continuous at every $x_0$: for $\\varepsilon > 0$ choose $\\delta = \\varepsilon$; the claim then follows from the reverse triangle inequality (Exercise 5.14).",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> $\\operatorname{sgn}: \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R}$ is not sequentially continuous at $x_0 = 0$: $x_n = \\tfrac1n \\to 0$, but $\\operatorname{sgn}(x_n) = 1 \\not\\to \\operatorname{sgn}(0) = 0$.<br><b>2.</b> Every function <i>from</i> a discrete metric space is continuous (choose $\\delta = \\tfrac12$, then $B_\\delta(x_0) = \\{x_0\\}$) &ndash; so continuity depends essentially on the metrics on $X$ <i>and</i> $Y$, not only on the formula of the map."
  },
  "k5_lemma_5_49": {
    title: "Lemma 5.49: Equivalence of the continuity notions",
    category: "5.4 Continuity",
    front: "Show that $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ continuity and sequential continuity at a point $x_0$ are <b>equivalent</b> (Lemma 5.49).",
    back: "<b>($\\Rightarrow$):</b> Let $f$ be $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ continuous at $x_0$ and $x_n \\to x_0$. For $\\varepsilon > 0$ choose $\\delta > 0$ with $f(B_\\delta(x_0)) \\subseteq B_\\varepsilon(f(x_0))$, and then $N$ with $x_n \\in B_\\delta(x_0)$ for $n \\ge N$. Then $f(x_n) \\in B_\\varepsilon(f(x_0))$ for $n \\ge N$, so $f(x_n) \\to f(x_0)$.<br><br><b>($\\Leftarrow$, contrapositive):</b> if $f$ is <i>not</i> $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ continuous at $x_0$, there is an $\\varepsilon > 0$ for which <i>every</i> $\\delta$ fails. For $\\delta = \\tfrac1n$ choose $x_n \\in B_{1/n}(x_0)$ with $f(x_n) \\notin B_\\varepsilon(f(x_0))$. Then $x_n \\to x_0$, but $(f(x_n))_n \\not\\to f(x_0)$ &ndash; $f$ is not sequentially continuous. $\\square$<br><br>One says briefly: $f$ is <b>continuous at $x_0$</b>; $f$ is called <b>continuous</b> if it is continuous at every point of $X$.",
    meta: "Script: Lemma 5.49, p. 247",
    explanation: "<b>Use:</b> two tools for the same notion. The $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ game is suited for <i>positive</i> continuity proofs; the sequential characterisation shines when <i>disproving</i>: a single sequence $x_n \\to x_0$ with $f(x_n) \\not\\to f(x_0)$ suffices. The &ldquo;trap sequence&rdquo; with $\\delta_n = \\tfrac1n$ is the standard recipe for that.",
    example: "Discontinuity of the Dirichlet function at every $x_0$: choose rational $x_n \\to x_0$ and irrational $y_n \\to x_0$ &ndash; the image sequences converge to $1$ and $0$ respectively, so $f$ cannot be continuous at $x_0$.",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> It is not enough to test only <i>special</i> sequences: for $f(x,y) = \\frac{xy}{x^2+y^2}$ (with $f(0,0) = 0$) $f$ converges to $0$ along both axes, but along the diagonal $x_n = y_n = \\tfrac1n$ it converges to $\\tfrac12$ &ndash; <i>every</i> sequence counts.<br><b>2.</b> In the contrapositive $\\varepsilon$ must stay fixed while $\\delta = \\tfrac1n$ shrinks &ndash; whoever lets both shrink at the same time proves nothing."
  },
  "k5_prop_5_50": {
    title: "Proposition 5.50: Characterisations of continuity",
    category: "5.4 Continuity",
    front: "Which four equivalent characterisations of (global) <b>continuity</b> of a function $f: X \\to Y$ does Proposition 5.50 give?",
    back: "For metric spaces $X, Y$ and $f: X \\to Y$ the following are equivalent:<br><br>(i) $f$ is continuous.<br>(ii) For every $x \\in X$, $f$ is $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ continuous at $x$.<br>(iii) For every $x \\in X$, $f$ is sequentially continuous at $x$.<br>(iv) <b>Neighbourhood characterisation:</b> for every $x \\in X$ and every neighbourhood $U \\subseteq Y$ of $f(x)$, the preimage $f^{-1}(U)$ is a neighbourhood of $x$.",
    meta: "Script: Proposition 5.50 & Exercise 5.51, pp. 247-248",
    explanation: "<b>Proof sketch (i) $\\Leftrightarrow$ (iv):</b> if $U$ is a neighbourhood of $f(x)$, it contains some $B_\\varepsilon(f(x))$; continuity gives $\\delta$ with $f(B_\\delta(x)) \\subseteq B_\\varepsilon(f(x)) \\subseteq U$, so $B_\\delta(x) \\subseteq f^{-1}(U)$. Conversely apply (iv) to $U = B_\\varepsilon(f(x_0))$.<br><br>(iv) is the &ldquo;topological&rdquo; formulation: it needs no metric values at all and is generalised in the 2nd semester to &ldquo;preimages of open sets are open&rdquo;.",
    example: "With (iii) one sees immediately: the composition of continuous functions is continuous &ndash; $\\lim g(f(x_n)) = g(f(\\lim x_n))$ by applying continuity twice (proof of Prop. 5.53 (i)).",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> The <i>image</i> direction would be false: continuous functions need not map open sets to neighbourhoods &ndash; the constant function $f(x) = 0$ maps every neighbourhood onto $\\{0\\}$, which is not a neighbourhood in $\\mathbb{R}$. The <i>preimage</i> is decisive.<br><b>2.</b> Exercise 5.51: from &ldquo;$(f(a_n))_n$ converges&rdquo; it does <i>not</i> follow that $(a_n)_n$ converges: $f(x) = x^2$ and $a_n = (-1)^n$ give $f(a_n) = 1 \\to 1$, but $(a_n)_n$ diverges &ndash; continuity transports convergence only <i>forwards</i>."
  },
  "k5_def_5_52": {
    title: "Definition 5.52: Uniform and Lipschitz continuity",
    category: "5.4 Continuity",
    front: "Define <b>uniform continuity</b> and <b>Lipschitz continuity</b> for $f: X \\to Y$ between metric spaces. How are the notions related?",
    back: "&bull; $f$ is called <b>uniformly continuous</b> if for every $\\varepsilon > 0$ there is a $\\delta > 0$ such that for <i>all</i> $x_1, x_2 \\in X$:<br>$$\\mathrm{d}_X(x_1, x_2) < \\delta \\implies \\mathrm{d}_Y(f(x_1), f(x_2)) < \\varepsilon$$<br>&bull; $f$ is called <b>Lipschitz continuous</b> if there is a <b>Lipschitz constant</b> $L \\ge 0$ with<br>$$\\mathrm{d}_Y(f(x_1), f(x_2)) \\le L \\cdot \\mathrm{d}_X(x_1, x_2) \\quad \\forall x_1, x_2 \\in X$$<br><br><b>Hierarchy:</b> Lipschitz continuous $\\implies$ uniformly continuous $\\implies$ continuous (both converses are false).",
    meta: "Script: Definition 5.52, p. 248",
    explanation: "<b>The decisive exchange of quantifiers:</b> for ordinary continuity $\\delta = \\delta(\\varepsilon, x_0)$ may depend on the point; for uniform continuity <i>one</i> $\\delta(\\varepsilon)$ must work everywhere. Lipschitz is even stronger: $\\delta = \\tfrac{\\varepsilon}{L}$ does this explicitly (for $L > 0$). On compact intervals every continuous function is automatically uniformly continuous (Theorem 3.75, generalised in the 2nd semester).",
    example: "$f(x) = 3x$ is Lipschitz continuous with $L = 3$. The distance function $x \\mapsto \\mathrm{d}(x, A)$ is 1-Lipschitz (Exercise 5.54). $\\sqrt{x}$ on $[0,1]$ is uniformly continuous (compact interval!), but <i>not</i> Lipschitz (infinite slope at $0$).",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> $f(x) = x^2$ on all of $\\mathbb{R}$ is continuous, but <i>not</i> uniformly continuous: $|f(x + \\tfrac1x) - f(x)| = 2 + \\tfrac{1}{x^2} > 2$, although the input distance $\\tfrac1x \\to 0$ &ndash; for $\\varepsilon = 2$ no fixed $\\delta$ survives.<br><b>2.</b> $\\sqrt{x}$ on $[0,1]$: uniformly continuous, but not Lipschitz &ndash; $\\frac{\\sqrt{x} - 0}{x - 0} = \\tfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x}} \\to \\infty$ breaks every constant $L$. Both inclusions of the hierarchy are therefore strict."
  },
  "k5_prop_5_53": {
    title: "Proposition 5.53: Constructing continuous functions",
    category: "5.4 Continuity",
    front: "Which five statements about the <b>construction of continuous functions</b> does Proposition 5.53 make?",
    back: "Let $X, Y, Z$ be metric spaces.<br><br>(i) <b>Composition:</b> if $f: X \\to Y$ and $g: Y \\to Z$ are continuous, then $g \\circ f: X \\to Z$ is continuous.<br>(ii) <b>Components:</b> $f: X \\to \\mathbb{C}^d$ is continuous if and only if all components $f_j = \\pi_j \\circ f : X \\to \\mathbb{C}$ are continuous.<br>(iii) The <b>addition</b> $+: \\mathbb{C}^2 \\to \\mathbb{C}$ is continuous.<br>(iv) The <b>multiplication</b> $\\cdot: \\mathbb{C}^2 \\to \\mathbb{C}$ is continuous.<br>(v) The <b>reciprocal function</b> $(\\cdot)^{-1}: z \\in \\mathbb{C}^\\times \\mapsto z^{-1} \\in \\mathbb{C}$ is continuous.",
    meta: "Script: Proposition 5.53, pp. 249-250",
    explanation: "<b>Proof highlights:</b> (i) via sequential continuity: $g(f(x_n)) \\to g(f(x_0))$ by applying continuity twice. (ii): the projections $\\pi_j$ are 1-Lipschitz ($|\\pi_j(v - w)| \\le \\|v - w\\|_\\infty$); conversely combine componentwise convergence with Prop. 5.44. (iii): addition is Lipschitz with constant 2. (iv)/(v): $\\varepsilon$-$\\delta$ arguments with the tricks from Prop. 5.30 (mixed term, &ldquo;$|z| \\ge |z_0| - |z - z_0| > \\tfrac{|z_0|}{2}$&rdquo;).<br><br><b>Consequence:</b> together with (i) and (ii) it follows that sums, products and quotients (with non-vanishing denominator) of $\\mathbb{C}$-valued continuous functions are continuous.",
    example: "Polynomials $p: \\mathbb{C} \\to \\mathbb{C}$ are continuous (built from addition, multiplication, constants and the identity). Rational functions are continuous on $\\{z \\mid q(z) \\neq 0\\}$ &ndash; via (v) and (i).",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> In (v) the restriction to $\\mathbb{C}^\\times = \\mathbb{C}\\setminus\\{0\\}$ is essential: at $0$ the reciprocal is neither defined nor continuously extendable ($\\tfrac{1}{1/n} = n$ explodes).<br><b>2.</b> The converse of (i) is false: from the continuity of $g \\circ f$ nothing follows about $f$ or $g$ individually &ndash; e.g. $g \\circ f$ is constant (hence continuous) whenever $g$ is constant, however discontinuous $f$ is."
  },
  "k5_uebung_5_54": {
    title: "Important Exercise 5.54: Distance functions",
    category: "5.4 Continuity",
    front: "Let $(X,\\mathrm{d})$ be a metric space and $A \\subseteq X$ non-empty. Show that $x \\mapsto \\mathrm{d}(x, A) = \\inf_{a \\in A} \\mathrm{d}(x, a)$ is <b>Lipschitz continuous with constant 1</b>.",
    back: "<b>Proof:</b><br>1. For arbitrary $x_1, x_2 \\in X$ and every $a \\in A$ the triangle inequality gives:<br>$$\\mathrm{d}(x_1, a) \\le \\mathrm{d}(x_1, x_2) + \\mathrm{d}(x_2, a)$$<br>2. Pass to the infimum over $a$ on the left (the right side is an upper bound for each of the infima):<br>$$\\mathrm{d}(x_1, A) \\le \\mathrm{d}(x_1, x_2) + \\mathrm{d}(x_2, a) \\quad \\forall a \\implies \\mathrm{d}(x_1, A) \\le \\mathrm{d}(x_1, x_2) + \\mathrm{d}(x_2, A)$$<br>3. Hence $\\mathrm{d}(x_1, A) - \\mathrm{d}(x_2, A) \\le \\mathrm{d}(x_1, x_2)$; swapping the roles gives the other direction and thus<br>$$|\\mathrm{d}(x_1, A) - \\mathrm{d}(x_2, A)| \\le \\mathrm{d}(x_1, x_2). \\quad \\square$$",
    meta: "Script: Important Exercise 5.54, p. 250",
    explanation: "The distance to a fixed set can change at most as fast as one moves oneself &ndash; the direct generalisation of the reverse triangle inequality ($A = \\{y\\}$, Exercise 5.14). Distance functions are an important source of continuous functions on arbitrary metric spaces (for example in separation arguments).",
    example: "$X = \\mathbb{R}$, $A = [0,1]$: $\\mathrm{d}(x, A) = 0$ for $x \\in [0,1]$, otherwise $= |x|$ resp. $|x - 1|$ &ndash; the &ldquo;distance ramp&rdquo;, obviously 1-Lipschitz (slope $\\pm 1$).",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> $\\mathrm{d}(x, A) = 0$ does <i>not</i> imply $x \\in A$: for $A = (0,1)$ and $x = 0$ we have $\\inf_{a \\in A} |x - a| = 0$, but $0 \\notin A$ &ndash; the infimum need not be attained.<br><b>2.</b> The constant 1 is optimal, one cannot do better: for $A = \\{0\\}$ in $\\mathbb{R}$, $|\\mathrm{d}(x_1, A) - \\mathrm{d}(x_2, A)| = ||x_1| - |x_2||$, which for $x_1, x_2 > 0$ is exactly $|x_1 - x_2|$ &ndash; no constant $L < 1$ works."
  },

  "k5_uebung_ultrametrik": {
    title: "Exercise (5.5.2): Ultrametrics and the p-adic metric",
    category: "5.5 Further learning material",
    front: "What is an <b>ultrametric</b>, what is the <b>$p$-adic metric</b> on $\\mathbb{Z}$, and what is the astonishing principle for balls in ultrametrics?",
    back: "&bull; <b>Ultrametric:</b> like a metric, but with the stronger inequality<br>$$\\mathrm{d}(x_1, x_3) \\le \\max\\{\\mathrm{d}(x_1, x_2),\\ \\mathrm{d}(x_2, x_3)\\}$$<br>Every ultrametric is in particular a metric ($\\max \\le$ sum).<br><br>&bull; <b>$p$-adic metric:</b> for a prime $p$ and $\\nu_p(x) = \\max\\{k \\mid p^k \\text{ divides } x\\}$:<br>$$\\mathrm{d}_p(x,y) = \\begin{cases} p^{-\\nu_p(x-y)} & x \\neq y \\\\ 0 & x = y \\end{cases}$$<br>Two numbers are &ldquo;close&rdquo; if their difference is divisible by a high power of $p$.<br><br>&bull; <b>Ball principle:</b> in an ultrametric <i>every point of a ball is its centre</i>: $x \\in B_r(x_0) \\implies B_r(x) = B_r(x_0)$.",
    meta: "Script: exercise (ultrametrics), Section 5.5.2, p. 252",
    explanation: "<b>Consequences of the principle:</b> all &ldquo;triangles&rdquo; are isosceles (the two longest sides equal), and two balls are either disjoint or contained in one another &ndash; a completely different geometry from $\\mathbb{R}^d$. The $p$-adic world is the foundation of large parts of modern number theory.",
    example: "$p = 2$: $\\mathrm{d}_2(0, 8) = 2^{-3} = \\tfrac18$ (since $8 = 2^3$), but $\\mathrm{d}_2(0, 3) = 2^0 = 1$. The sequence $a_n = 2^n$ converges $2$-adically to $0$!",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> The standard metric on $\\mathbb{R}$ is <i>not</i> an ultrametric: $\\mathrm{d}(0,2) = 2 > \\max\\{\\mathrm{d}(0,1), \\mathrm{d}(1,2)\\} = 1$.<br><b>2.</b> Intuitions from $\\mathbb{R}$ fail: $2$-adically $2^{10}$ is &ldquo;tiny&rdquo; ($\\mathrm{d}_2(0, 2^{10}) = 2^{-10}$), while the number $1$, small in absolute value, has $2$-adic distance $1$ from $0$ &ndash; size and $p$-adic closeness are completely decoupled."
  },
  "k5_uebung_rangmetrik": {
    title: "Exercise (5.5.2): Rank metric on matrices",
    category: "5.5 Further learning material",
    front: "How is the <b>rank metric</b> defined on the set $X$ of $m \\times n$ matrices over a field $\\mathbb{K}$, and how does one prove the triangle inequality?",
    back: "$$\\mathrm{d}(A, B) = \\operatorname{rank}(A - B)$$<br><b>Verification of the axioms:</b><br>&bull; <i>Definiteness:</i> $\\operatorname{rank}(A - B) = 0 \\iff A - B = 0 \\iff A = B$.<br>&bull; <i>Symmetry:</i> $\\operatorname{rank}(B - A) = \\operatorname{rank}(-(A-B)) = \\operatorname{rank}(A - B)$.<br>&bull; <i>Triangle inequality:</i> with $A - C = (A - B) + (B - C)$ and the subadditivity of the rank $\\operatorname{rank}(X + Y) \\le \\operatorname{rank}(X) + \\operatorname{rank}(Y)$ from linear algebra:<br>$$\\mathrm{d}(A,C) \\le \\mathrm{d}(A,B) + \\mathrm{d}(B,C). \\quad \\square$$",
    meta: "Script: exercise (rank metric), Section 5.5.2, p. 252",
    explanation: "A nice example of how results from linear algebra (subadditivity of the rank: the image of $X + Y$ lies in the sum of the images) directly yield a metric. The rank metric takes only the integer values $0, 1, \\dots, \\min\\{m,n\\}$ and is used, among other things, in coding theory.",
    example: "$A = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix}$, $B = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}$: $\\mathrm{d}(A,B) = \\operatorname{rank}\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} = 1$.",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> $\\mathrm{d}(A,B) = |\\operatorname{rank}(A) - \\operatorname{rank}(B)|$ would <i>not</i> be a metric: two different matrices of the same rank would have distance $0$ (definiteness fails).<br><b>2.</b> Convergence in the rank metric is drastic: since all distances are integers, as in the discrete metric a sequence converges if and only if it is eventually constant &ndash; there is no &ldquo;coming ever closer&rdquo; here."
  },
  "k5_uebung_aequivalente_metriken": {
    title: "Exercise (5.5.2): Equivalent metrics",
    category: "5.5 Further learning material",
    front: "When are two metrics $\\mathrm{d}_1, \\mathrm{d}_2$ on $X$ <b>equivalent</b> (with constant $C$), and what follows for convergence?",
    back: "&bull; <b>Definition (eq. 5.4):</b> there is a constant $C > 0$ with<br>$$\\frac{1}{C}\\, \\mathrm{d}_1(x, y) \\le \\mathrm{d}_2(x, y) \\le C\\, \\mathrm{d}_1(x, y) \\qquad \\forall x, y \\in X$$<br>&bull; <b>Consequence:</b> a sequence converges with respect to $\\mathrm{d}_1$ if and only if it converges with respect to $\\mathrm{d}_2$ &ndash; with the same limit. (Because $\\mathrm{d}_2(x_n, x_0) \\le C\\,\\mathrm{d}_1(x_n, x_0) \\to 0$, and conversely.)",
    meta: "Script: exercise (5.4), Section 5.5.2, p. 252",
    explanation: "$C$-equivalent metrics distort distances only by a bounded factor &ndash; convergence, continuity and openness remain identical. Prototype: $\\mathrm{d}_\\infty, \\mathrm{d}_1, \\mathrm{d}_2$ on $\\mathbb{R}^d$ are pairwise equivalent with $C = d$ resp. $C = \\sqrt{d}$ (inequalities (5.2)/(5.3)) &ndash; exactly what lies behind Proposition 5.44.",
    example: "$\\|w\\|_\\infty \\le \\|w\\|_1 \\le d\\,\\|w\\|_\\infty$: the metrics induced by $\\|\\cdot\\|_1$ and $\\|\\cdot\\|_\\infty$ are $d$-equivalent on $\\mathbb{R}^d$.",
    counterexample: "<b>1.</b> The same convergence notion does <i>not</i> force $C$-equivalence: $\\mathrm{d}_1(x,y) = |x-y|$ and $\\mathrm{d}_2(x,y) = \\frac{|x-y|}{1+|x-y|}$ on $\\mathbb{R}$ have the same convergent sequences, but $\\mathrm{d}_2 < 1$ is bounded, $\\mathrm{d}_1$ is not &ndash; no $C$ satisfies $\\mathrm{d}_1 \\le C\\,\\mathrm{d}_2$.<br><b>2.</b> Non-equivalent metrics with different convergence: the standard metric vs. the discrete metric on $\\mathbb{R}$ &ndash; $\\tfrac1n \\to 0$ holds only for the first."
  }
};
